Part 1: What is ALM? 

Definition (One-liner): Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) is the end-to-end process of governing, developing, testing, deploying, and maintaining apps in a structured way throughout their life1 2. 

The 3 Pillars of ALM: 

Governance & Planning: Setting rules, managing environments & permissions, tracking changes, and ensuring compliance/safety (like version control, DLP policies). 

Development & Testing: Designing, building, and continuously integrating changes, then validating that everything works as expected (functional, performance, security tests)3. 

Maintenance & Operations: Deploying updates, monitoring apps in production, fixing issues, and incorporating feedback for improvements (closing the loop back to planning). 

ALM Lifecycle Stages: As a cycle, ALM encompasses Plan → Build → Test → Deploy → Operate → Feedback (then back to Plan, iteratively). At each stage, ALM ensures roles, tasks, and tools are in place so nothing falls through the cracks. 

Why ALM Matters: Without ALM, development can become chaos – no version control, lost changes, “it works on my machine” issues, manual errors moving to production4 5. ALM brings discipline: code moves through dedicated environments (no building directly in production, no “cowboy changes”), and you can roll back if needed. In short, ALM means predictable, high-quality delivery instead of surprises. 

🍕 Analogy: ALM is like running a pizza restaurant. 🍕 You don’t take an order and immediately hand raw dough to the customer (like directly editing production)! Instead, you have stages: plan the menu (requirements), prepare the dough in a kitchen (development), have a chef taste it or test it (QA), then serve to the customer (production). If something’s wrong at any stage, you fix it before moving on. ALM ensures every pizza/app is baked properly before it’s delivered. 

🧠 Memory Trick: Remember “App’s Life Managed” – ALM ensures an app’s entire life is managed from creation to retirement. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: ALM is not just source control or one tool; it’s a whole process. A common exam pitfall is to think ALM = using Git or Azure DevOps only. Actually, ALM covers environment strategy, solutions, processes, and governance beyond just code versioning6. If the exam asks “What does ALM include?”, recall it’s people + process + tools (governance, dev, ops), not just storing code. Also, be ready for ALM stage-related questions – e.g., identifying that you need a separate test environment to validate a deployment before production7. Exam might list steps out of order; remember Plan->Build->Test->Deploy as the logical flow. 

 

Part 2: Environment Strategy 

Definition: A Power Platform Environment is a separate space to store and manage apps, data, flows, and resources – like a sandbox or container for a given project or stage8. Environments isolate different solutions so that development and testing can happen without affecting production users9. 

All Environment Types & Usage: Power Platform offers five main types of environments (plus a special Teams variant). Here’s a quick comparison: 

Environment Type 

Purpose & Characteristics 

Security & Access 

Default 

Automatically created per tenant for personal use, lightweight dev/trial (no backup, not for production)10. All licensed users are makers here by default. 

Limited control: You cannot restrict maker access (everyone’s a Maker)11. Don’t build critical apps here – it’s meant for experiments. 

Production 

Long-term environment for live business apps, fully managed & backed up12. Requires available capacity to create. Use for any environment on which you deeply depend. 

Full control: Admin-managed. Only designated makers can create/modify apps. Supports manual backup/restore. 

Sandbox 

Non-prod environment for Dev or Test, with features like reset and copy (to refresh from Prod)13. Can be converted from Prod or back as needed (admin toggle). 

Full control: Treat as staging ground. Use for integration testing or UAT. Makers need maker role; user access can be limited for testing. 

Trial 

Temporary environment for short-term evaluation or POC. Auto-expires after 30 days (extendable by admin)14. One per user typically. 

Full control while active. Good for demos or trying preview features, but not for sustainable work (it will vanish!). 

Developer 

Personal Dev environment with a Developer Plan license. Each user gets one for building and testing privately15. Doesn’t consume your paid capacity. 

Limited control: Only the owner has access by default (others can’t be easily added)16. Ideal for individual exploration. 

(Note: There’s also Dataverse for Teams environments (auto-created when you build a Power App in Teams). These are lightweight and confined to Teams context – fewer features, limited ALM capabilities, no multi-environment solutions. For serious enterprise ALM, use standard environments, not Teams ones.) 

Minimum ALM Pipeline: The golden rule of ALM is to have at least two separate environments: one for developing changes and one for running production (live) apps17. Optimal is a three-stage pipeline: DEV → TEST (or UAT) → PROD18. 

Dev (Development) – for building and iterating unmanaged solutions. Multiple team members might develop here. 

Test/UAT (Quality Assurance/User Acceptance) – for managed solution imports from Dev, where you test the deployment and have users verify new features in a controlled setting19. 

Prod (Production) – the live environment where end users work. Only thoroughly tested, approved managed solutions reach here. 

Simple ASCII diagram of flow: 

Optionally, large organizations might also include SIT (System Integration Test) or Pre-Prod/Staging for complex scenarios, but Dev→Test→Prod is the core. 

When to use Sandbox vs Developer vs Production: 

Use Sandbox as your shared Dev or Test environment if multiple people collaborate. E.g., Team ALM approach: each dev might have a personal environment or branch, but integrate to a shared Sandbox for combined testing. Also, use Sandbox for UAT because it can be reset or mimic Prod via copy without risk to Prod data. 

Developer env is great for individual sandboxing – quick tries, personal projects, or when you don’t want to spin up a full sandbox (since it’s free per user). It’s isolated to you, which avoids stepping on colleagues’ toes. However, you cannot share developer env with testers easily. 

Production env is strictly for final deployment. Treat it as sacred: no direct editing by makers (except emergency hotfix with caution), minimal maker access (only solution importers). It’s locked down to run stable apps for real users. 

🔥 Important Rules / Best Practices: 

Never build in the Default environment for official projects. It’s a free-for-all zone (everyone’s a Maker)20 with no reliable backup; using it for key apps invites chaos. Instead, create dedicated Prod and Dev environments for any serious solution. 

Don’t mix roles in one environment: Dev should be separate from Prod to avoid accidents (e.g., testing an app in Prod by mistake). Similarly, avoid doing UAT in Prod. 

Plan environment naming & capacity: Use clear names (e.g., ProjectX-Dev, ProjectX-Test, ProjectX-Prod) so it’s obvious which is which. Ensure you have available Dataverse capacity for new envs (1GB min for Prod creation)21. 

Align environment versions: Ensure Dev environment is on same or earlier platform version as Prod, to avoid import issues22 – you can import a solution to a newer version environment, but not reliably to an older version one23. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Setting Up a Simple Env Strategy: 
Goal: Create a Dev and Test environment to practice ALM. 

In Power Platform Admin Center, click + New Environment. Create a Sandbox environment named “ALM-Dev” with a Dataverse database. Assign yourself Environment Maker and System Admin roles. 

Similarly, create another Sandbox (or Production type if you have spare capacity) named “ALM-Test”. 

In ALM-Dev, navigate to Settings > Users + permissions to ensure you have the correct roles (you should as creator). In ALM-Test, add any test users or adjust Maker permissions if needed. 

Now you have a mini pipeline: you can build an app/flow in ALM-Dev, package as solution, and try importing to ALM-Test as if it’s UAT. This mimics a Dev→Test flow. 
Outcome: Two isolated spaces ready for you to practice exporting/importing solutions, without risking production. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Many PL-200 questions focus on “where to do what”. Traps include: 

Thinking it’s okay to develop in Production (it’s not!). The correct answer is usually develop in a separate environment, then deploy to Prod24. 

Confusing environment types. E.g., if asked “Which environment auto-expires?” the answer is Trial. Or “Which environment type has the special constraint that all users are makers?” – that’s Default25. 

Default vs Production usage: If the exam scenario describes a mission-critical app and the option says “Use the Default environment,” that’s a red flag (likely incorrect). 

At least one test environment: Microsoft’s recommended ALM includes a test environment26; if an answer omits any test stage, be wary. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Establish an Environment Strategy early in any project. Get stakeholder buy-in for the number of environments (more envs = more overhead, but safer). Use security groups to control who can create environments or who has maker access in each. Also consider using Managed Environments (a feature that imposes extra governance like weekly digest, DLP enforcement) for Prod or critical envs. And always set up Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies — e.g., block connectors like social media in Prod environment if not needed. This is part of environment governance that complements ALM. 

 

Part 3: Solutions – The Container 

Definition: A Solution is a packaging mechanism for Power Platform customizations — essentially a container that holds all the components of your app/flow so you can move them between environments and manage versioning27. Solutions are the core of ALM in Power Platform28; they let you bundle everything needed (apps, flows, tables, etc.) to deploy or back up changes in one file. 

What Goes Inside a Solution: Any component that can be customized can (usually) be part of a solution29. Common solution components include: 

Dataverse Tables (including their columns, relationships, forms, views, business rules), 

Canvas Apps and Model-Driven Apps, 

Power Automate Flows (cloud flows), 

Chatbots (Power Virtual Agents), 

Custom Connectors, PCF code components, Web Resources (HTML/JS/CSS files), 

Business Process Flows, Security Roles, Option Sets (Choices), etc. 
Basically, if you built it or customized it, you add it to the solution so it can be tracked. The solution is like a manifest listing all included components and their definitions. 

What Doesn’t Go in a Solution: Solutions do not include actual business data (records) or certain environment-specific bindings. For example: 

Data records (like rows in a table) aren’t moved by solutions. Use data migration or Configuration Migration tool (see Part 15) for that. 

Connections to external services (SP, Outlook, etc.) aren’t directly inside solutions; instead, you include Connection References (pointers to a connection, see Part 9) that must be mapped to actual connections upon import30. 

Environment Variable values (see Part 8) – the solution carries the definition of the variable and potentially a default value, but each environment has its own Current Value that is not overwritten on import31. 

Environment-specific settings (like user roles assignments, DLP policies, or AI Builder models tied to environment) – those are managed outside of solutions. 

Default Solution vs Custom Solutions: 

Power Platform comes with built-in default solutions. The “Default Solution” (and a “Common Data Service Default Solution” in older terminology) contains everything in the environment by default32. If you customize something outside a custom solution, it ends up in the Default solution. This is NOT ideal for ALM. 

Custom Solutions – any solution you create (with a custom name & publisher). Best practice is to ALWAYS develop inside a custom solution33. Why? Because default solution is like the “master pile” – if everyone worked there, you couldn’t separate your changes for deployment or revert them easily. Custom solutions let you modularize changes, source-control them, and export/import as needed. 

🎯 Key Concept: The solution you create is effectively your project’s container. All custom components (apps, flows, tables) should reside in a solution so they can be neatly packaged. Without a solution, you can’t move customizations properly – that’s akin to trying to copy random pieces of an app one by one. Solutions bring order. 

🍕 Analogy: Solutions are like shipping containers for your app’s parts. 📦 Imagine trying to ship a car overseas by mailing all its individual parts separately – chaotic and risky! Instead, you pack them in one big container and seal it – that’s the solution. Similarly, solutions bundle all pieces (like apps, flows, resources) so they travel together34. It keeps things from getting lost and ensures the “shipment” arrives intact on the other side (target environment). 

🔥 Important Rule: Work in a custom solution from Day 1. Don’t build components outside of a solution (which would drop them into the Default solution unwittingly). Microsoft’s guidance: “Makers should use their own unmanaged solutions.”35 If you must explore something quickly, fine, but as soon as it’s an actual requirement, create a solution for it. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Establish a solution naming convention and strategy early. For example, you might have solutions per feature or per release. E.g., “SalesApp_CoreComponents” vs “SalesApp_Reports” or separate solutions for different modules of a big project. This can help in large projects for dividing work. Just avoid scattering too much; coherence is key. Also, use the “Set Preferred Solution” feature so new components default into your chosen solution (preventing the “oops, I made it in default” problem)36. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Create and Use a Solution: 

In your Dev environment (make.powerapps.com), navigate to Solutions → + New solution. Give it a name like “ProjectX – Main Solution”, choose (or create) a publisher (we’ll discuss publishers in Part 5), and version (start with 1.0.0.0). Create it. 

Now add an existing app or create a new component inside this solution: e.g., within the solution, click New → App → Canvas app (or any component of your choice). Build a simple app or add an existing table via Add existing. 

Observe that the component now shows under your solution. It’s “contained”. Make a small change (like adding a column to a table, or a button on the app) – it’s tracked inside the solution. 

Export the solution: On the solution’s command bar, choose Export → (select Unmanaged for now). This gives you a .zip file containing all those components. 

(Optional) If you have a Test environment, try Importing that solution zip there (Solutions → Import). See that your app and components come through. 
Result: You’ve packaged and moved components via a solution – the foundation of ALM. By practicing this, you’ll understand the flow of making changes in Dev and moving them to other envs. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

Default Solution usage: If the exam question scenario describes a maker building something important and one answer says “use the default solution,” that’s almost certainly wrong37. The correct approach is using a custom solution with a publisher. 

Solution Content: A tricky question might ask, “What’s not included when you export a solution?” Correct answers: data records, connections (only references), and current environment variable values (only definitions & default values move)38. Also, security role assignments to users aren’t part of solution. 

Cross-environment issues: They might test that flows in a solution require connection references to work after import. So remember, a flow will be disabled after import until you map its connection references to actual connections (the UI usually prompts). If asked, “After importing a solution with a flow, it’s not working; what should you check?” – likely connection references need mapping or the flow needs enabling. 

 

Part 4: Managed vs Unmanaged Solutions 

Definition: Unmanaged Solutions are for development (open and editable), while Managed Solutions are for distribution to test or prod (locked, packaged)39. They are two flavors of the same solution: unmanaged = editable “source” version, managed = read-only “deliverable” version. 

Unmanaged Solutions (Dev): In Dev, you work in an unmanaged solution. It’s like a working folder – you can add/remove components and tweak freely40. If you delete an unmanaged solution (container), the custom components stay in the environment (they just become part of Default solution)41. Unmanaged is considered your source-of-truth (you check these into source control, etc.)42. 

Managed Solutions (Deployed): When you’re ready to ship, you export your unmanaged solution as a managed package. That managed solution is imported to Test/Prod. Managed solutions are sealed – you cannot directly modify their components in target (unless you purposely layer an unmanaged customization on top)43. If you uninstall a managed solution, all components it brought are removed from the environment44. Managed solutions can’t be exported (to protect IP)45. Think of managed as the compiled/binary version of your solution and unmanaged as the source code. 

Side-by-Side Comparison: 

 

Unmanaged Solution 😃 (Dev) 

Managed Solution 🔒 (Deployed) 

Editable? 

Yes. You can add, remove, change components directly46. 

No (read-only). Can’t edit components directly in it47. Changes must come via a new solution version or layering. 

Where to use? 

Development environments only48. It’s your working solution. 

Test/UAT/Production (non-dev) envs49. You import managed there to deploy. 

Contains what? 

All components you added. (Often everything needed for a feature/app.) 

Same components as unmanaged source had, but in locked form. 

Exportable? 

Yes. Can export as unmanaged (for backup/source) or as managed (to create deployable)50. 

No. You cannot re-export a managed solution from an environment51. 

Delete behavior 

Deleting unmanaged only deletes the solution container; components remain as customizations in default solution52. 

Uninstalling managed removes all its components from environment (and their data!)53. 

Layer position 

Forms the Unmanaged layer (top) in Dev environment54. 

Forms part of Managed layers in target. (Multiple managed solutions can stack)55. 

Delete Behavior (Critical): This is a huge difference: 

If you delete an unmanaged solution, you’re basically just removing the grouping label. Every component you created is still in the environment (sitting in the Default solution)56. So, nothing breaks – you just lose that tidy container. 

If you delete a managed solution (i.e., uninstall it from an environment), you remove everything it brought, potentially including custom tables (with their data) or custom fields (with their data)57. Once gone, it’s as if those customizations never existed (unless they were layered by another solution). This can nuke production data inadvertently. ⚠️ Always double-check before deleting a managed solution and prefer upgrade paths to remove components (see Part 7). 

Where to Use Each: Unmanaged in Dev always58. Managed in any environment that acts as downstream (Test/Prod)59. It’s considered best practice to never have unmanaged customizations in Prod; Prod should mirror exactly what’s delivered via managed solutions, for consistency. 

You Can’t Create a Managed Solution Directly: The only way to get a managed solution is by exporting an unmanaged one as managed60. You build and iterate in unmanaged form, and produce managed output as a build artifact. If you try to make a change in Test/Prod, you have to either do an unmanaged layer override or go back and update the unmanaged solution in Dev, then re-export a new managed version. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Export & Import (Managed vs Unmanaged): 
Scenario: You have the custom solution “ProjectX – Main Solution” in Dev from Part 3. Now you’ll export it as managed and test behavior. 

In Dev environment, open Solutions, select your “ProjectX – Main Solution”. Click Export. Choose Managed (which will default to publishing everything and then generating a managed zip). Save the managed .zip locally. 

Switch to your Test environment. Import the .zip (Solutions → Import). After import, you should see the solution listed with a Managed tag. Notice you cannot edit the app/flow inside (you can open to play, but there’s no edit button or you get a read-only message). 

Still in Test, try to delete a component delivered by that solution (like delete the custom table or app via maker portal). The platform should prevent you because it’s part of managed solution (it might say remove the solution instead). Now, go ahead and Delete the managed solution via Solutions → select solution → Delete. Confirm the prompts. 

After deletion, verify that the custom components are gone. For example, the app shouldn’t appear in Apps list, and if you had a custom table, it’s removed (and any data in it is deleted!). 
Result: You saw that a managed solution is locked down and that uninstalling it cleanly removes its contents – a stark difference from unmanaged, where removal leaves components intact. This highlights careful planning needed for managed deployments. 

🍕 Analogy: Unmanaged vs Managed = Blueprint vs Finished Building. 🏗️ In development, you’re working with blueprints (unmanaged) – you can scribble, erase, and redraw freely. Once done, you hand over a constructed building (managed) to the client – it’s solid, and they can use it but not easily change the structural beams without calling you back. If something’s wrong, you return to the blueprint, adjust, then update the building. And if the client demolishes the building (uninstall managed solution), everything built is gone. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

The exam often asks about the consequences of uninstalling solutions or where to use managed vs unmanaged. Remember: Uninstalling a managed solution removes everything it contained61. If a question states “Deleting an unmanaged solution will delete all components” – that is false (trap!). Unmanaged deletion only deletes the container, not the components62. 

Also, “Where can you import a managed solution?” – answer: not back into the environment it originated from (Dev) if the unmanaged exists there63 (the system won’t allow a managed and its base unmanaged in same env). 

Another typical scenario: “A solution in prod needs a quick hotfix. Should you directly edit it?” — No, you should go to Dev, do the fix in the unmanaged solution, export a new managed version and import. Direct edits in Prod create an unmanaged layer which is an anti-pattern (only do in emergencies, then reconcile later). 

💡 Consulting Tip: Always keep an unmanaged “source” copy of your solution (in Dev or in source control). The managed solution in prod is not editable and not extractable back to source. If you lose your unmanaged source and only have managed in prod, future changes become painful (basically rebuilding from scratch). So treat your unmanaged solution file as the crown jewels – back it up (export regularly, or better yet, store in a repo via solution packing tools). Another tip: turn off “allow unmanaged customization” via Managed Properties on components if you want to really enforce no one changes things in managed solutions even by accident. This prevents monkey patches on top of your managed solution which could complicate upgrades later. 

 

Part 5: Solution Publisher & Prefix 

Definition: A Solution Publisher is an identifier for who created the solution and comes with a unique prefix that stamps all components created under that publisher64. The publisher and prefix help manage ownership and avoid naming collisions in Dataverse. 

What is a Publisher? It’s basically metadata: a publisher has a name (e.g., “Contoso Ltd”) and a prefix (e.g., “contoso” or an abbreviation)65. When you create a solution, you assign a publisher. That publisher’s prefix will prepend new components’ schema names (like new tables, fields, etc.). It’s also used to track dependencies by publisher (since one solution can extend another if same publisher). 

What’s the Prefix & How It Works: The prefix is a short string (often 3-5 characters) that gets automatically added to all new entity/field names you create in that solution. For example, if prefix = “contoso”, a new table “Order” becomes schema name contoso_Order. This ensures that if two different ISVs both create a “Order” table, their prefixes keep them distinct66. Importantly, the prefix only auto-applies to new components; if you add existing ones, their schema name stays as is, but any new field you add to them will have prefix appended. 

Why NEVER use Default Publisher (“new_”): The default publisher (used if you use the environment’s default or don’t specify) has prefix “new”67. If you see components named new_customfield, that’s a sign the default publisher was used. It’s considered bad practice because everyone’s default prefix is “new”, which offers no uniqueness – collisions can happen, and it looks unprofessional. Also you can’t change those names later. So always create your own publisher with a clear, unique prefix (like your company or project code). 

Prefix is Permanent: Once a component is created with a prefix, you cannot change it later68 (short of deleting and recreating the component). That’s why planning your publisher prefix up front is crucial. E.g., if you start with prefix “abc” and decide later you wanted “xyz”, too late for existing components. 

Naming Best Practices: Use a prefix that’s likely unique to your organization or project. Many use company initials (e.g., for Contoso, maybe cts or cto). Keep it lowercase and short. The publisher name itself can be friendly (e.g., “Contoso ALM Team”). Also, one organization might create multiple publishers for different teams if needed, but often one org-level prefix suffices for internal projects. 

🍕 Analogy: Prefix as a surname for components. If components are “children” and publisher is the “family”, the prefix is like giving each child the family last name. Two different families might each have a child named “John”, but their surnames differentiate them (John Smith vs John Doe). Similarly, prefix ensures your “Account” table is contoso_Account, distinct from someone else’s litware_Account. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Create a Custom Publisher: 

Go to Solutions and click Publisher (often a sub-menu or separate area) → New Publisher. Name it e.g. “ProjectX Solutions, Inc.” and give a prefix like “prjx” (the form will auto-suggest based on name, but you can edit it). Save the publisher. 

Now create a new Solution and choose this custom publisher. 

Inside that solution, create a new Table (Dataverse table) called “Ticket”. After creation, check its schema name in Table settings or solution view. It should show something like prjx_Ticket as the schema (prefix applied). 

Add a new Column “Status” to that table. Similarly, it will likely be named prjx_status behind the scenes. 

You can even open Advanced Find or Solution XML (if you export) to see the prefix on components. 
Outcome: You have a solution with a unique prefix, and you can see how it marks everything. This demonstrates how prefix stamping works and prevents name collisions with other publishers. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

A likely exam question: “What’s the purpose of a solution publisher prefix?” The answer: to provide unique schema name prefixes to avoid naming conflicts69. 

They might also ask: “How do you ensure two solutions don’t conflict in names?” – By using different publishers/prefixes. 

Another trap: “True or False: You can change the prefix of a publisher after components are created.” The answer is False70. Once used, prefix stays with those components for life. 

Also note, “What prefix do apps created in default environment use by default?” – “new_” (for default publisher). Recognize that means default publisher was used (not good in professional ALM). 

💡 Consulting Tip: Many organizations standardize on one publisher (prefix) for all internal development. This can simplify planning but has a downside: if you ever want to merge solutions or have layered solutions, having everything under one publisher might limit layering (because two managed solutions from same publisher can’t be layered on top of each other for certain components easily). For most, that’s fine. But consider at least having separate publishers if you envision releasing separate modules that could be installed independently. Also, occasionally ISV solutions might require their base solution (with their publisher) then you create an extending solution with your publisher on top – that’s okay. Just avoid mixing default publisher anywhere. Lastly, keep the publisher info (name, prefix) professional; it shows up in solution XML and can be seen by others if you share your managed solutions. 

 

Part 6: Solution Layers 

Definition: Solution Layers represent the stacked order of different solutions’ changes on a component71. In Dataverse, multiple solutions (managed and unmanaged) can modify the same element; layers determine which change “wins” at runtime. The top layer’s value usually takes effect. 

Layer Hierarchy & Order: Think of layers as transparent overlays: 

At the bottom is the System Layer (base platform definitions). 

Above that, any Managed Solutions you install stack in the order imported (last imported = topmost managed layer)72. 

At the very top is the single Unmanaged Layer for all customizations done directly in the environment (including any unmanaged solutions in Dev, or unmanaged customizations on top of managed)73. 

Essentially, unmanaged layer always sits above managed layers in a given environment, and among managed ones, the newest is on top74. When something is removed from a top layer (e.g., you uninstall a managed solution), the change beneath (next layer down) becomes active again75. 

“Top Wins” Rule: If two different layers define the same property (say two solutions both change a field’s label), the topmost layer’s change wins (applied at runtime)76. Remove that layer (like uninstall that solution or remove the unmanaged customization), then the next one’s effect will show. This is how layering allows progressive enhancement or override of behavior. 

Merge Behavior: Not all components simply override. Some are merged if possible – notably Model-driven App navigation, Form customizations, Site Maps have merge logic77. For example, two managed solutions adding fields to a form might both show their fields if no conflict, or if conflict, last one wins. But most components (e.g., flows, canvas apps, optionsets) use top-wins (the top layer fully overrides lower)78. The nuance for managed layered solutions is beyond PL-200 depth, but know that only a few component types merge. 

Viewing Solution Layers: In the Power Apps maker portal, you can check layers: e.g., in a solution’s component list, select a component and click “See solution layers”79. This shows the stack of where that component’s definition is coming from – very handy to troubleshoot “why is my field label X in prod when my solution says Y” (maybe an unmanaged customization layer in prod is still on top). The interface highlights active vs inactive layers (inactive meaning a removed/uninstalled solution that still shows as a layer historically but not impacting runtime). 

Unmanaged Customizations Layer: All unmanaged changes (whether from an imported unmanaged solution or manual tweak in environment) share one layer called “Active” or “Unmanaged Customizations”80. If you have multiple unmanaged solutions, they don’t stack separately – they all contribute to that one top layer. That’s why if you have more than one developer working unmanaged, their changes just co-exist (or override each other) in that single layer. It’s also why using source control to manage your unmanaged changes is crucial, because merging in environment is not segregated by solution. 

Removing Layers: When you remove a solution (uninstall managed or remove unmanaged customizations), you effectively peel off that layer. The platform then falls back to whatever is below. This is how, for example, uninstalling a managed patch or solution reveals either the previous solution’s version or the default behavior. If all layers above system are removed, you’re back to out-of-the-box defaults. 

🍕 Analogy: Solution layers are like layers in Photoshop (or transparent acetates). 🎨 The bottom layer is the original image (system), then each solution adds a transparency on top with changes. The top-most change is what you see. If you remove the top transparency, the change beneath becomes visible. Some layers might merge (like blending certain elements), but often the top just covers the bottom if they draw at same spot. The concept is the same for Dataverse components layered by solutions. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Inspect Solution Layers: 

If you did the Part 4 exercise (imported a managed solution and then perhaps layered an unmanaged change on it?), use that scenario. If not, do a quick setup: In a Dev environment, create a table “Sample” with a field “Name” and set its display name to “Name”. Export as managed (v1.0). Import to Test. Now change the display name of “Name” field in Test environment directly (create an unmanaged layer by editing in Test; or import an unmanaged solution to Test that changes it). 

In Test, go to Solutions → find that “Sample” table (likely in Default solution or the imported one) → select the Name field → click See solution layers. 

You should see something like: System (base), [Your managed solution layer], and Unmanaged layer (active) on top. The unmanaged layer’s Display Name is what shows in the UI due to top-wins. 

Remove the unmanaged layer by deleting the unmanaged customization (in classic, one can “Remove Active Customization” or re-import solution). Then check the layers again – top layer might now be the managed one, meaning its name shows. 
Outcome: You got a visual of layering. This helps you understand how multiple solutions or direct changes interplay – vital for debugging ALM issues. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

They might quiz: “What happens if you import a managed solution that changes something already changed by another managed solution?” Answer: The new solution’s changes form a layer on top (so new solution wins for that component)81. 

Or: “How can you see which solution’s change is currently applied to a component in an environment?” – By checking solution layers in the environment’s solution explorer (the UI to view layers)82. 

Also be aware of a classic one: “Which components support merge behavior vs override in layering?” The ones to remember: model-driven app navigation, forms, sitemaps support merge; most others, top layer simply wins83. If an exam scenario describes two solutions adding fields to a form, they might both appear (merge), whereas two solutions setting a field’s requirement level – the last one’s setting wins (top layer for that property). 

💡 Consulting Tip: Keep layers clean in Prod when possible. Unmanaged layer on top of managed in Prod often means someone changed something directly in Prod – try to avoid this because it complicates future solution upgrades (the system won’t remove a managed solution if something depends on it in unmanaged layer). If needed, document any “temp” unmanaged fixes in Prod and incorporate them back into the Dev solution soon. Also, when layering multiple managed solutions (like some modular solutions that depend on base ones), plan the publisher and layering logic carefully. Typically, solutions from different publishers can overlay, but if a solution from Publisher A extends components from another solution by Publisher A, you must release an updated base or patch instead (since one publisher’s solutions considered one chain). This is advanced, but important if you deliver modular managed solutions. 

 

Part 7: Import, Export, Upgrade, Update, Patch 

Exporting Solutions: When you Export your solution from Dev, you choose Unmanaged or Managed. Unmanaged export is used for backup/source control, or moving to another dev environment. Managed export is used to create the package for Test/Prod deployment84. You can only export if you have appropriate rights (Environment Admin or System Admin for managed export). 

Importing Solutions: When importing a solution into an environment where that solution (or an earlier version) already exists, you get important choices85: 

Upgrade (Full upgrade): This is the default for managed solutions. It brings the solution to the new version, and removes any components that were present in the old version but not in the new version86. Use this when you want the target to exactly match the source solution’s state (with obsolete components cleaned up). 

Stage for Upgrade: Similar to upgrade, but delays deletion of removed components by installing the new version alongside the old (the old one gets a _Upgrade suffix) until you click “Apply Upgrade” later87. Use this if you need to perform some manual data migration or checks before dropping the old components. Once ready, you apply the upgrade to remove the old solution and components88. 

Update: This option imports the new solution, but does NOT remove any components that were removed in the newer version89. It effectively overwrites existing components with new versions but leaves orphans (like if you deleted a field in Dev, it remains in Prod after update). Update is faster and simpler (one step) than upgrade, but can lead to leftover elements if you removed stuff in Dev90. Use update when you want to preserve any data or parts temporarily, or if performing quick fixes that don’t involve removing elements. 

Comparison – Upgrade vs Update vs Stage: 

Method 

Removes Deleted Components? 

Process 

Use case 

Upgrade (Managed) 

Yes, deletes components not in new version (cleans up)91. 

Two-step: import with upgrade option, then complete (either immediately or via stage/apply)92. 

Ideal for major releases where you removed/renamed items. Ensures environment isn’t cluttered by old stuff. 

Stage for Upgrade 

Delays removal until you apply upgrade manually93. 

Imports new solution as separate layer (_Upgrade), old still present until “Apply Upgrade” done94. 

When you need a transition period (e.g., move data from old component to new one) before finalizing removal. 

Update 

No removal – old components not present in new remain in target95. 

One-step import (no second step). Leaves environment with possibly extra components. 

For minor changes or quick version bump where you didn’t remove anything, or want to keep legacy component/data for now. Also if speed matters. 

Remember: If it’s the first time you import (no existing version in target), these options aren’t applicable – it’ll just import normally as new. 

What is a Patch: A Patch is a special mini-solution that contains only the changes (deltas) for a parent solution version96. You create a patch to deliver a bug fix or small update without full solution upgrade. Patches stack on top of the base solution’s layer and can be rolled up into an upgrade later97. However, Microsoft no longer recommends using patches for most scenarios98. Instead, the guidance is to simply create a new version of the solution (with a higher version number) and import as an upgrade or update. Patches can complicate layering and are mostly legacy approach. 

Why Patches are Not Recommended (anymore): Patches introduced complexity and got replaced by simpler flows. Now with easily clonable solutions and pipeline automation, it’s often easier to increment your solution version and do an upgrade. Patches can’t remove components and sometimes confused makers. Microsoft’s own docs say “Using patches isn’t recommended.”99. 

Holding Solutions and Staged Upgrades: When you Stage for Upgrade, you effectively have two versions side by side (base and _Upgrade). This is called a “holding” pattern – the system is waiting for you to finalize. During this time, you should perform whatever required (like migrating data from old field to new field if removing it). Once done, you click Apply Upgrade which removes the old solution and merges patches100. If something goes wrong, you could theoretically cancel by removing the new and keeping old. This adds complexity, so use stage only if necessary. 

Apply Upgrade vs Delete/Reimport: If you have staged, Apply Upgrade finalizes it properly (gives a neat upgrade removal). If you instead manually delete the old solution without applying, or some odd sequence, you could leave junk. So follow the intended process. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Version Upgrade: 
Goal: Experience update vs upgrade difference by doing two releases of a solution. 

In Dev, open your solution (say version 1.0.0.0). Make a minor change (e.g., add a field or change something). On solution Details, update the version number to 1.1.0.0 (Power Apps forces you to pick new version on export or you can set it manually). Export as managed (v1.1). 

In Test (which currently has v1.0 from previous import), click Import with the new v1.1 managed. On import dialog, choose Update (not Upgrade) in Advanced settings. Complete import. 

Verify that new changes are there, and also any component you removed in Dev is still present in Test (because update didn’t remove it). 

Now import the same 1.1 solution again into another environment (or delete from Test and re-import fresh) but this time choose Upgrade on import. After import, verify the old version’s components are gone (if you removed any in v1.1). 

Optionally, try Stage for Upgrade: Re-import v1.0, then import v1.1 with Stage option. See that solutions list shows both v1.0 and an “_Upgrade” solution. Then click Apply Upgrade on v1.0. It should remove v1.0 entirely leaving only v1.1. 
Result: You have seen how Update left extras whereas Upgrade cleaned up the old stuff. Understanding this ensures you choose correctly in real deployments. 

🧠 Memory Trick: For Upgrade vs Update, remember U-pgrade = Uproot (it uproots old stuff) vs U-pdate = Uber-safe (it leaves everything, so safer if uncertain). And Patch = Piece (a small piece on top). 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

If asked “Which import option removes components that are no longer in the solution?”, answer Upgrade101. If asked which doesn’t remove them, that’s Update102. 

They might present a scenario: “You removed a field in Dev solution, and want to ensure it’s removed from Prod after import. What to do?” – The answer: increase version and do a solution upgrade (or stage+apply) so that field is removed in Prod103. 

Patches: A question might be conceptual like “What’s a patch best used for?” Answer: Small interim fixes on a managed solution, which can later be rolled up into a higher version solution104. But also recall Microsoft doesn’t prefer them now (the exam might not emphasize that, but PL-200 might just test that you know patches exist and general use). 

Also, to create a patch, your base solution version must be published and you create patch 1.0.1 etc. You can have multiple patches. Patches merge when doing an upgrade (like 1.0.1 + 1.0.2 become part of 1.1.0.0). 

One more: If you try to import a solution with a lower version than what’s installed, it will fail. So you must always increment version for updates/upgrades105. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Decide on an internal versioning strategy for solutions (we talk more in Part 16). Many teams use Major.Minor to represent release vs sprint, and Build/Revision for smaller increments or patches. For example, a major release might bump from 1.4 to 2.0 after a big milestone. Use upgrade for releases where old stuff should be cleaned, use updates for simpler additive changes if nothing removed. Avoid piling up too many patches; rather, do a clean version upgrade periodically because patches can become messy if numerous. Also automate solution import in pipelines (with pac CLI or build tools) to ensure correct options are used (like always do upgrade in prod to avoid leftover). 

 

Part 8: Environment Variables 

Definition: Environment Variables are placeholders for configuration values that can differ by environment, enabling you to adjust settings (like URLs, keys, feature flags) without modifying the app/flow logic106. They consist of a Name, a Value (current), and an optional Default Value. 

Why They Matter: They solve the problem of environment-specific constants. For example, your Dev environment might call a Dev API endpoint, but Prod should call Prod API. If you hard-coded that in a flow or app, you’d have to edit on each environment – bad for ALM. Instead, you use an env variable like “API URL”, which you set differently in each environment107. When you move the solution across, you simply change the variable’s value in the target env. 

Default Value vs Current Value: 

Default Value is part of the env variable definition (the solution component) and travels with the solution export108. It’s like a fallback or suggested value. 

Current Value is a separate entity, essentially the actual value in a given environment, often not meant to be transported109. If current value exists, it overrides default. Current values do NOT automatically carry over with a solution import – typically you set them manually per environment (or via pipelines/DevOps scripts)110. The idea: the solution contains the “slot” for the config; each environment fills in its own value. 

Example: You have env var “API URL”. Default value = “https://dev.api.com/” (set in Dev). In Test env, you import the solution – if you don’t set a current value, the default might still point to dev.api (which is not correct for test). So you then set current value in Test to “https://test.api.com/”. In Prod, maybe set to “https://api.contoso.com/”. 

Types of Environment Variables: Several data types are supported111: Text (string), Number, Yes/No (two options), JSON (for structured configs), Data Source (for referencing environment-specific data sources, like a SharePoint site or SQL server), Secret (for sensitive values – integrated with Azure Key Vault)112. Data Source type and Secret type are special: 

Data Source type ties to a connector + connection parameter (e.g., a SharePoint site URL). It doesn’t store the credential, just the parameter needed to differentiate environments. 

Secret type optionally allows you to link to an Azure Key Vault secret – so you manage the secret outside of Power Platform and env var holds a reference. Great for ALM because in each environment you can link to that env’s Key Vault secret without storing it directly in solutions. 

How Env Vars Travel in Solutions: The definition (name, type, default value) is included in the solution. The current values are not included unless you explicitly add them (which is usually not desired for secrets or environment-specific values). Typically, you’d leave current values out so that when importing, you will be prompted (in UI or pipeline) to enter the appropriate values for the new environment113. The modern import experience lets you provide values during import for any env variables in the solution114. 

Best Practice: Use environment variables for any config that can change per environment – common ones: URLs (like SharePoint site, API base), GUIDs (for specific records that differ between envs), file paths, feature toggles, and credentials (with Secret type). This centralizes config, making deployments smoother (just set new values rather than editing flows). Also, document them so admins know to update them post-import. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Environment Variables ALM: 

In Dev environment, open your solution and Add New → Environment Variable. For example, create one named “Email_Sender_Address” of type Text, with Default Value = “donotreply-dev@contoso.com”. (Simulate that dev uses a dummy sender email). Save it. 

Build a small flow or app in the solution that uses this env variable (for a real scenario, maybe a flow sending email picks up “From” address from the variable). 

Export the solution (managed). In Test environment, import it. During import, note the UI might show the env variable and allow setting a value. If not, after import, go to Solutions → find env variable component, open it and set Current Value to “donotreply-test@contoso.com”. 

Check that your flow/app in Test now uses the Test address because you set current value. 
Outcome: You see that the solution moved the “Email_Sender_Address” key (with default maybe as dev’s value, if you included it), but you had to or could override it to an appropriate one in Test. This demonstrates how environment-specific data is handled. 

🧠 Memory Trick: Default = Delivery (comes with the solution), Current = Customize per environment. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

Common Q: “What happens to environment variable values when you export a solution?” – Only Default value goes (as part of the definition)115; current values do not travel (they remain in source env and not applied to target)116. So if you rely on a current value in Dev that’s different from default, you or the importer must set it in target. 

“How to handle sensitive configuration in ALM?” – Use Secret-type env variables with Azure Key Vault (so the actual secret isn’t in the solution)117. 

“Environment variable vs Connection Reference differences?” – Env var is for a value you want to use in apps/flows; Connection Reference (Part 9) is specifically about a connector’s identity/connection. 

They might also test that environment variables are solution-aware (you add them to solutions just like any component) which distinguishes them from earlier attempts like environment-specific manual settings. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Manage environment variables carefully in ALM: prepare a checklist for deployments that includes “Set environment variable X, Y, Z in target after import”. If using pipelines, you can preconfigure environment variable values mapping for each stage (so it auto applies them). Also consider storing a JSON or CSV of environment specific values in your repository (but not secrets in plain text! use Key Vault for those). A “config file” approach combined with pac CLI environment variable import can reduce manual steps. And always have safe defaults – e.g., maybe default points to a non-production endpoint or is blank, so that if someone forgets to set current, it doesn’t accidentally point to something unintended. 

 

Part 9: Connection References 

Definition: A Connection Reference is a solution component that acts as a placeholder for an actual connection to an external service within an app or flow118. It decouples the logic (flow/app) from the specific connection instance, enabling ALM by mapping to different connections in each environment. 

Why Connection References Exist: In old days, if you exported a flow that used, say, a SharePoint connector, you’d have to rewire it to a SharePoint connection in the new environment. Now, a connection reference is like a pointer named “MySPConnectionRef” inside the solution. In Dev env, that ref points to your Dev SharePoint connection. When you import solution to Test, the flow comes with a placeholder “MySPConnectionRef” – you then map it to a Test environment’s actual SharePoint connection credential. This way, flows and canvas apps remain environment-agnostic regarding connections. 

How They Work: When you add a flow or canvas app to a solution, any new connection it uses will prompt you to create a connection reference (unless one exists to reuse). The connection reference has a name and points to a specific connection object in that environment. On export, the solution keeps the reference name, not the actual credentials. On import, the system will attempt to auto-map if a connection with same name exists, or prompt you to choose an existing connection or create a new one to satisfy that reference. For example, if the solution has a reference to a SQL connection, you must have a SQL connection in target environment and associate it. 

Connection vs Connection Reference: 

A Connection is the actual authenticated link to a service (like "Contoso SharePoint connection using Alice’s credentials"). It is not solution-aware (lives in environment). 

A Connection Reference is a solution component that refers to that connection. You might think of it as an alias inside the solution for the needed connection. The important separation is that the solution doesn’t bring over any sensitive connection info, just a stub that must be fulfilled by a local connection. 

Mapping on Import: The UI during solution import will list all connection references and ask you to select which existing connection in the target env should map to each reference. If none exists, you might need to create one on the spot. E.g., if your solution’s connection reference expects a SQL Server connection, you need to have a corresponding connection in the target (under your account or a service principal) to map. This mapping is a one-time setup per environment for that ref. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Connection References in Action: 

In a Dev environment, in a solution, create a Canvas App or Flow that uses a connector (for example, a SharePoint action that reads an item). When you add that action, it’ll ask to pick a connection (or create one). If it’s the first time using that connector in the solution, you’ll see it creates a connection reference (likely named like “[YourName] SharePoint”). Save the app/flow and solution. 

Export the solution as managed. In a Test environment, ensure you have a valid SharePoint connection set up (with perhaps a test user account). 

Import the solution into Test. You’ll be prompted to map the SharePoint connection reference to an actual connection (your test connection). Do so and finish import. 

Open the app/flow in Test – verify it’s properly connected (likely you’ll see it shows your test connection in the settings). 
Outcome: This shows that connection references let your apps and flows find the right connector in each environment. Without them, the flow would break in the new environment because it wouldn’t know who to connect as. 

🧠 Memory Trick: Connection Reference = Connector Placeholder. It’s like a phone’s contact name. The contact (connection ref) stays same, but in each environment you assign it the actual phone number (connection) relevant there. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

If asked “How do you ensure a flow in a managed solution can connect to a required service in a new environment?” – answer: add a Connection Reference for that service in the solution and map it on import119. 

They might test that connections themselves are not part of solutions. So if an answer suggests “export the connection as part of solution”, that’s wrong – you can’t export actual connections, only references. 

Also note: connection references are needed for any Power Automate flows in solutions that use connectors (most flows do), and for canvas apps that use connectors except Dataverse. If you forget to include a connection reference, the flow won’t import properly, but nowadays the maker portal auto-adds them when you add the flow. 

For exam, just recall: connections are environment-specific; connection references orchestrate that in solutions. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Use service accounts or service principals for connections in higher environments. E.g., in Dev, makers might use their personal connections to test. But in Prod, ideally you map connection references to a service account’s connection (so that flows don’t break if the maker leaves). Plan with your IT that these accounts or app registrations exist. Also, keep naming consistent – e.g., name the connection references clearly (“SP_ContosoSiteConnection”) so it’s obvious what to map in each environment. That way the pipeline or the admin knows which actual connection to choose. If using Power Platform Pipelines (Part 13), you can even pre-set which connection reference uses which connection via pipeline config, further smoothing ALM. 

 

Part 10: Solution Checker (Quality Gate) 

Definition: The Solution Checker is a built-in static code analysis tool that scans your Power Platform solution for any performance, stability, security, or best practice issues120. It’s like a lint or analyzer for power apps/flows, helping catch potential problems early. 

What It Does: When you run Solution Checker on a solution, it inspects components like your canvas app formulas, flows, plug-in code, etc., against a set of rules. Examples of checks: 

App performance: e.g., a canvas app loading too many large images or using inefficient formulas. 

Flow reliability: e.g., loops in flows lacking termination, or actions that commonly cause performance slowdowns. 

Environment & Supportability: e.g., usage of deprecated connectors or preview features that might break. 

Code issues: if you have custom code (plugins/PCF), it can highlight using outdated SDK calls. 

Security/accessibility: e.g., if a form lacks recommended accessibility patterns. 

Severity Levels: Issues are flagged typically as Critical, High, Medium, Low, or Informational. Critical/High should be addressed before production as they indicate likely performance or functionality issues; lower might be optimization suggestions. 

When to Run It: Best practice is to run Solution Checker before every major export or deployment (so essentially when a solution is nearing release) as a quality gate. Many incorporate it into their ALM pipelines (like in DevOps build pipeline, run the checker automatically). It’s also available from the maker portal (where you can manually trigger it)121. 

How to Run It: In the Power Apps solution interface, select a solution and click “Solution checker” → “Run”122. It will queue an analysis (cloud-based). After a bit, refresh or click to see Results. You get a list of issues with links to docs or details on how to fix them. Alternatively, you can use the Power Apps CLI or DevOps build tasks to run it programmatically. It requires an environment with Dataverse and an account with appropriate rights. 

Common Issues Caught: e.g., “Canvas app contains 100+ controls on one screen (performance)” or “Flow runs too frequently” or “Plug-in uses ExecuteMultiple incorrectly causing slow performance”, etc. Another example: a check might find you have an entity set to track auditing but no retention policy – it could warn about potential log growth. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Run Solution Checker: 

In Dev environment, ensure you have a solution with at least a couple components. On the Solutions page, select your solution. On the command bar, click Solution checker → Run. 

Wait a couple of minutes then refresh or go to Solution checker → View results. If your solution is small, you might see no issues or some informational ones. For demonstration, you could intentionally add a simple canvas app with an unnecessarily complex formula to trigger a warning (but that’s advanced). 

Observe the results: if any appear, note their severity and suggested fix. 
Outcome: Even if there are no findings, you’ve learned the process to run the checker, which is valuable for ensuring quality. 

🎯 Key Concept: Solution checker is basically your QA automated code review. It’s not infallible, but it’s a helpful baseline for catching known patterns of poor quality. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

Expect a question like: “What is the purpose of Solution Checker?” – Answer: to identify issues in your solution (performance, reliability, etc.) before deployment123. 

They might ask, “When should you run solution checker?” – ideally prior to exporting/importing solutions, as part of ALM process. Or specifically, “How to ensure a solution follows Microsoft best practices?” – by running solution checker. 

Also, “What can solution checker analyze?” – It covers canvas apps, model-driven components, flows, environment variables usage, etc. It doesn’t directly fix issues; it reports them. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Make solution checker an approval gate – e.g., require that there are no Critical/High findings before a solution moves to UAT/Prod. If any, have devs address them or justify why not. Over time, you’ll see recurring suggestions (like optimizing formulas or adjusting controls usage); incorporate those best practices into your dev standards. Also know that solution checker rules get updated by MS; keep an eye on new rules that might flag previously acceptable design. And if you have isolated false positives or acceptable exceptions, document and possibly use the checker’s rule suppress (if available) or just note it. It’s one piece of a quality assurance puzzle (in addition to user testing, performance testing, etc.). 

 

Part 11: Segmented Solutions 

Definition: Segmented Solutions refer to packaging only a subset of components (particularly parts of a table) in a solution, rather than whole tables or all components. This allows more granular deployment of changes, often called table segmentation124. Essentially, you include only what changed. 

What They Are: Traditionally, if you added one new form to a table, exporting that table in a solution pulled everything of that table by default. Segmenting means you intentionally choose only the new form (and maybe needed pieces), not the entire table’s forms/views, thereby keeping the solution smaller and avoiding overwriting other parts unnecessarily125. This results in a lean solution update that doesn’t include unchanged components. 

When to Use Them: In large projects with big tables, or multiple teams working on different parts of the system, segmenting helps reduce collisions. E.g., Team A can deploy an update with just a new view on the Account table, without packaging the entire Account table which might override Team B’s form changes in their own solution. It’s also useful for performance: importing huge solutions takes longer, so smaller segmented ones are faster for minor updates. 

How to Create a Segmented Solution: When adding a table to a solution, the UI gives options126: 

No Objects Selected: (just minimal table info, essentially placeholder)127. 

Include All Objects: everything including all forms/views (only do this if you are deploying a brand new table to an env that doesn’t have it)128. 

Include Table Metadata: includes table settings but no sub-components129. 

Edit Selected Objects: (Recommended) – you manually pick which forms, views, fields, etc., to include that are relevant to your change130. For example, if you only changed one form, select just that form (the system auto includes necessary metadata like table itself). This way you don’t ship unrelated components. 

This feature ensures you don’t unintentionally add layers on components you didn’t change131 (preventing the scenario where you override existing changes with a blank or older version if included by accident). 

Benefits for Large Projects: If multiple overlapping solutions target the same table, segmentation avoids each deployment from affecting parts of the table they shouldn’t. It also reduces that nasty “unnecessary dependency” problem – where including whole table might require including other stuff. By segmenting, you make solutions more independent and modular132. 

⚠️ Caution: If a target environment doesn’t have a given table at all, you must include all objects of that table (the full definition) or the import will fail (because the table itself is missing)133. So segmentation is mainly for updates to existing tables in the target env. 

🎯 Key Point: Segment only updated components, not everything. This preserves layering and reduces risk of stomping on other changes134. 

🍕 Analogy: Segmented solutions are like sending only the changed chapters of a book to the editor. You don’t re-send the whole 500-page novel if you only edited chapter 3 and 7. You send those chapters. Similarly, segmented solutions let you ship just the parts that changed, not the entire “book” (table) again. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Use segmentation to create “feature solutions”. For example, for a big central table like Accounts, you might have one solution that primarily owns it, but if other teams need to add something small, they could use segmented solutions. But careful: multiple teams editing one table can get complicated no matter what; coordinate with other teams about which forms/fields each is responsible for. Also, always test in a merge environment to ensure layering outcomes are what you expect (especially for forms merging multiple changes). 

 

Part 12: Solution Dependencies 

Definition: Dependencies are relationships where one component requires another to function135. In solution terms, dependencies ensure you can’t delete or import something without the pieces it relies on. 

What They Are: For example, if a form uses a field, the form is dependent on that field. That means you cannot remove that field until you either remove or modify the form first, or the system will block you136. Dependencies exist both within a solution (component to component) and across solutions (solution to solution). 

Required vs Optional Dependencies: 

Required dependency: A component absolutely needs another. E.g., a lookup field is useless without the table it references – so table is required. 

Optional dependency: Not as rigid, maybe more like it exists but not mandatory? (In practice, most dependencies are required for something to function). 

Checking Dependencies: In the solution UI, if you try to remove or delete a component that has dependencies, it will warn and list them. Also, you can explicitly click Show Dependencies on a component to see what other components rely on it137. For example, a table might show dependencies like views, forms, flows that reference it. 

Common “Cannot Delete” Scenarios: If you try to delete a field that’s on a form or used by a flow, you’ll get a message like “Cannot delete component due to dependencies”. The remedy is to remove or alter the depending component first. The dependency viewer will list what’s blocking138. This ensures stability – you can’t accidentally break something by removing a needed piece. 

Solution Dependencies (Between Solutions): If Solution B depends on a component in Solution A, you cannot import B without A present, or cannot remove A while B is still installed139. Managed solutions sometimes come in sets like that (a base solution and an add-on that depends on it)140. The platform checks the solution manifest for any references to other solution components and enforces the correct order. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Exploring Dependencies: 

In Dev, within a solution, create a new field (column) on a table and place it on a Form. Perhaps also reference that field in a View. Now try to remove the field from the solution. The system should stop you, saying it’s in use. 

Click the error details or use Show Dependencies on that field. It will list dependencies like the form and view you added it to. 

Remove the field from the form and view (or delete those components) until the dependency check is clear. Now remove the field. 

Also try this: If you have two managed solutions installed, attempt to uninstall one that others rely on. E.g., if you have a managed solution that introduced a custom table and another managed that adds a field to that table (thus second depends on first), try removing the first – it will refuse until the second is removed first141 142. 
Outcome: You see the protective mechanism of dependency tracking in action, preventing you from inadvertently breaking things. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: 

A question might describe a scenario: “A user cannot delete a column because of dependencies. What could be causing this?” The answer: another component (form, view, workflow) uses that column, so you must remove those references first143. 

“What happens if you try to uninstall a base managed solution while a dependent solution is present?” – It will fail; you must remove the dependent solution first144. 

Also know conceptually: dependencies exist to maintain integrity; any referenced component must have its dependencies satisfied at import (or removal triggers dependency error). 

🎯 Key Tip: Always carefully plan component removal and check dependencies in your dev solution before trying to import to target. If you remove something from the solution in Dev but it’s still present in Test environment from a previous version (like a field you want to delete), you must import as an Upgrade (Part 7) to properly remove it. If you do an update, that removed field remains, and if you ever try to delete it later in Test, you might hit dependencies if something is using it. So it’s best to remove all references in Dev before releasing. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Keep solutions tidy – avoid adding components you don’t need (to prevent spurious dependencies), and remove components from solutions only when you truly need to deprecate them. If you need to delete a component that’s widely used, coordinate that carefully (maybe with data migration to a new component). Also, for big changes consider doing them in steps – e.g., first release may remove references (like remove a field from forms/flows but keep it in DB), and a second release to delete the field itself, to minimize risk. 

 

Part 13: Power Platform Pipelines 

Definition: Power Platform Pipelines are a built-in ALM feature (since late 2022) that provides a simple, UI-driven way to automate moving solutions from Dev to Test to Prod, directly within Power Platform145. It’s designed to “democratize” ALM so that even citizen developers can push their changes in a controlled pipeline without needing deep Azure DevOps knowledge146. 

Pipeline Stages: Typically, an admin sets up a pipeline with defined stages (e.g., Dev -> UAT -> Prod). Each stage is linked to a specific environment. Then, makers can go to their solution and hit Deploy (as seen in solution command bar) and choose a pipeline stage to push to147. The pipeline handles exporting the solution as managed from source and importing into target automatically. 

Setup (Admin side): First, an admin configures a Pipeline Host environment (a special env that holds pipeline configurations)# Part 1: What is ALM? 
Definition: Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) is the end-to-end process of managing the life of an application from initial design to development, deployment, and ongoing maintenance148. In other words, ALM ensures that applications are planned, built, tested, deployed, and managed in a consistent and controlled manner. 

ALM’s Three Pillars: ALM is often divided into three interconnected disciplines that work together: 

Governance: Plan and Control. This covers defining requirements, making design decisions, managing resources, setting standards, tracking changes, and ensuring compliance and security149. 

Development: Build and Test. This entails actual design, coding (or configuring in low-code), and testing of the application’s components150. It involves iterating on features, fixing bugs, and aligning with requirements. 

Maintenance/Operations: Deploy and Monitor. Releasing applications to production, monitoring performance, handling user feedback, supporting users, and scheduling updates or improvements over time. 

Lifecycle Stages: ALM spans multiple stages in a continuous loop – think Plan → Build → Test → Deploy → Operate → Monitor → Feedback. It’s a cycle where each deployment leads to new learning and planning for future enhancements. The stages ensure quality and improvement at each step. 

Why ALM matters: Without ALM, development is chaotic – versions get mixed up, changes aren’t tracked, and deployments break. Imagine shipping a complex product (like a car) without assembly lines, quality checks, or manuals – that’s what app development without ALM looks like 🔧 ALM adds structure and quality control to app development151, reducing errors and ensuring that as apps evolve, changes are deliberate and reversible. It’s about delivering reliable applications consistently. 

🍕 Analogy – ALM as a Pizza Kitchen: Picture a pizza restaurant kitchen: you have a chef (developer), a recipe (requirements plan), an oven for baking (test environment), and the serving to customers (production deployment). If the chef just tosses ingredients without measuring (no planning) or serves without tasting (no testing), the pizza could be inedible. ALM is ensuring the recipe is followed, the pizza is tested before it goes to the customer, and adjustments are made for next time. Just as a great pizzeria runs on consistent processes (prep, cook, quality check, serve), ALM keeps app development consistent and high-quality. 

🧠 Memory Trick: Remember ALM as “Always Launch with Methodology.” It’s a reminder that every time you launch a new app or update, you should follow a method or process (ALM) instead of winging it. 

🔥 Important Rule: Address all three ALM pillars. Don’t focus only on building the app (development) and forget governance or maintenance. For instance, always set up environments for dev/test (governance) before building, and plan how you’ll support the app after going live (maintenance). Neglecting one pillar can break the entire cycle – like having an untested app (missing testing stage, causing user issues). 

⚠️ Exam Trap: PL-200 might test “what falls under ALM?”. Common misunderstanding: ALM is not just source code management or DevOps pipelines, it’s the entire lifecycle (planning, building, deploying, managing)152. Remember that solutions (Power Platform’s packaging mechanism) are central to ALM, and environment management is a key part of governance in ALM. If you see a question implying ALM = only version control, that’s a trap – emphasize complete lifecycle and environment management. 

 

Part 2: Environment Strategy 

What is an Environment? In Power Platform, an environment is a container for apps, data, flows, and other resources for a specific purpose153. It isolates resources from each other – you might have separate environments for development, testing, and production to prevent changes and data from interfering. Environments define boundaries for security, permissions, and data; they ensure that, for example, a test app only connects to test data, not production data154. 

Types of Environments (Power Platform offers several types, each designed for certain uses155): 

Environment Type 

Purpose & Characteristics 

Security & Access 

Default 

Automatically created for the tenant. Meant for personal exploration and trial apps, not critical production use156. Lacks manual backup options and has all users as makers. 

Minimal control: All users can create apps by default157. Cannot be deleted or fully secured. ⚠️ Never build important production apps here. 

Production 

Persistent environment for long-term, live business apps. Requires available capacity to create158. Use for actual business operations data. 

Full control: Admin-managed. Supports backup & restore, user roles, etc. Only designated makers build in Prod. 

Sandbox 

Non-production environment for dev or test, with extra admin features like Reset and Copy159. Use for building and testing apps isolated from Prod. 

Full control: Typically accessible to developers (Environment Maker role). Can be converted to Production and vice versa as needed160. 

Trial 

Temporary environment for short-term testing or proof-of-concepts. Expires after 30 days (extendable)161. Good for quick evaluations. 

Full control while active: But auto-cleanup means not for lasting projects. Limited 1 per user typically. 

Developer 

Personal dev environment available via Developer Plan. Intended for individual’s building and testing (one per user)162. 

Limited control: Only the owner has access by default; cannot add other users via security groups163. Doesn’t expire as long as you use the Developer Plan regularly. 

Minimal ALM Setup: At least two environments (Dev and Prod) are needed for basic ALM: you build in one and deploy to the other. Recommended: use three – Dev → Test → Prod164. This ensures you have a Test/UAT environment to validate the solution deployment and functionality before impacting production users165. Some large organizations use more (like a specialized UAT, pre-prod, training environment, etc.), but always keep Prod separate. 

ASCII Flow: Develop in DEV → validate in TEST → release to PROD. 

When to Use Each Environment Type: 

Use a Sandbox or Developer environment for development. If multiple team members are co-developing an app, a shared Sandbox environment is suitable (everyone is an Environment Maker on that Sandbox). For solo development or personal practice, a Developer environment suffices. 

Use Production environment only for what end users actively use (production apps, live data). No direct building or experimenting here. 

Default environment should be avoided for enterprise ALM (it’s open to all users, lacks backup control166). Only use it for personal or initial training apps if needed, but not for official projects. 

Trial environment is useful to temporarily try something new (like preview features or quick POCs) separate from your main environment chain. Never rely on it for anything that must persist. 

🔥 Important Rules (Environment Strategy Best Practices): 

Never develop or test in Production. Always make changes in Dev first, then promote via solutions. This prevents experiments from accidentally breaking live apps. 

Don’t build business-critical solutions in Default environment. The default environment is a “shared sandbox” where everyone can modify things167. Use dedicated environments for any important project. 

Name environments clearly (e.g., “Finance App – Dev”, “Finance App – UAT”, “Finance App – Prod”) so their purpose is obvious. 

Sync environment versions: Ensure your Dev environment’s Dataverse version is not higher than Prod’s, otherwise solution import might fail due to version mismatch168. You can import solutions into newer-version environments, but not reliably into older ones169. 

Managed Environments (optional): Consider using Managed Environments (a Power Platform feature) for Prod (and possibly Test). They provide admin controls and extra telemetry for governance. However, they require premium licenses for all environment users. 

📍 Practical Exercise: Set Up a Dev/Test/Prod Environment Strategy 

Create a Dev environment: In the Power Platform Admin Center, create a new Sandbox environment (e.g., “ProjectX-Dev”). Enable a Dataverse database. 

Create a Test environment: Another Sandbox environment (e.g., “ProjectX-Test”). 

Use an existing Production (or create one if needed) for Prod (e.g., “ProjectX-Prod”). 

Assign roles: Add your makers to the Dev and Test environments as Environment Makers. Only give Prod access to a limited set of users (like a few admins or the deployment lead for solution import). 

Build an app in Dev: Create a simple Canvas app or solution component in Dev environment, then practice exporting it as a solution and importing into Test and then Prod (we’ll cover the solution steps in upcoming sections). 

Outcome: You’ll have a working pipeline of separate environments – a safe “kitchen” (Dev), a “taste-test table” (Test), and the “dining room” (Prod) 🍽️. This isolation is fundamental to applying everything else in ALM. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Microsoft exams often ask about environment usage. One tricky scenario: “Where should makers build and test apps?” Correct answer: In a development (or sandbox) environment, then a separate test/UAT environment, before production170. Also remember: the Default environment is not for production or official dev – it’s a common mistake to build there, so if an exam question suggests doing serious development in Default, it’s likely wrong. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Treat environments as long-term assets. Plan capacity (each Prod env needs Dataverse storage, etc.) and commit to maintaining them. For example, routinely backup critical environments or use managed environments for Prod to get extra control. Also, set up Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies per environment early – e.g., stricter in Prod (block connectors that shouldn’t run in Prod), more open in Dev for testing connectors. A well-designed environment strategy is the backbone of ALM and prevents cross-contamination of data and configurations. 

 

Part 3: Solutions – The Container 

What is a Solution? A Solution is a package that groups together customization components (apps, flows, tables, etc.) for moving, managing, and maintaining them across environments171. It’s the cornerstone of Power Platform ALM – you make changes inside a solution in Dev, then export that solution and import it into Test/Prod to apply those changes. Solutions also allow versioning and layering of changes. 

🎯 Key Concept: A solution is like a shipping container for your Power Platform changes172 – instead of shipping loose items (untracked customizations), you pack them in a container (solution) with a label (version, publisher) so they can be delivered intact to another environment. 

Contents of a Solution: Solutions can include almost every component you create in Power Platform: 

Dataverse objects: Tables (and their columns, relationships, forms, views, business rules, etc.), Choices (option sets), processes (Business Process Flows, Workflows), security roles, model-driven app site maps, etc. 

Apps: Canvas apps, Model-driven apps, Power Pages websites. 

Automation: Power Automate flows (cloud flows), Power Virtual Agents (bots), AI Builder models. 

Connectors and Code: Custom connectors, PCF controls (code components), web resources (HTML/JS files), plug-ins (custom code compiled in assemblies). 

Configuration artifacts: Environment variables (definitions), connection references. 

If it’s a part of your application’s structure or logic, you typically can include it in a solution. (These are known as “solution components”173.) 

What is not in a Solution: Solutions do not carry actual business data (records) – e.g., accounts or invoices in Dataverse, or files, or SharePoint list contents are outside the solution. Also: 

Connections themselves aren’t in the solution; instead, you use Connection References to point to connections (we cover that in Part 9). 

Environment Variables’ values (the “Current Value”) do not travel with the solution. Only the definition (with a Default Value possibly) is in the solution; you set environment-specific values after import174. 

User accounts, user permissions are environment-specific and not moved by solutions. 

Data needed for configuration (lookup lists, etc.) must be moved via other means (Part 15 discusses the Configuration Migration tool). 

Default vs. Custom Solutions: 

Default Solution – Every environment has a special Default Solution (and a “Common Data Service Default Solution”) that contains all components (including those created outside custom solutions)175. The default solution uses the Default Publisher (prefix new_). While you can make changes directly here, it’s not ALM-friendly. It’s like a giant basket of all customization, making it hard to isolate or transport changes. Changes made outside of any custom solution end up in the Default solution by default, which leads to unmanaged clutter. 

Custom Solution – A solution you create (with a custom name and publisher). Best practice is to always develop inside a custom solution. This allows you to export just that solution’s components, collaborate with version control, and avoid surprising side effects. You can have multiple custom solutions in an environment (for modular projects or separate modules of a big project). 

Why Always Use Custom Solutions: 

Clarity: All your changes are grouped logically. The default solution quickly becomes a mess where it’s unclear what’s part of which feature. 

Portability: You can’t export the default solution or parts of it easily, but with custom solutions you export exactly what you need and import to other environments. 

Layering: When you import a managed solution to target, it sits on top of any existing solutions as a layer (Part 6 covers layering). If you never use custom solutions, all customizations are in one layer (default) which is not maintainable. 

Publisher Prefix: Custom solutions use a custom publisher (e.g., prefix contoso_ or abc_), ensuring that your custom components have unique schema names (no collisions). Default’s new_ prefix is generic and screams “unmanaged customization”. 

📍 Practical Exercise: Creating and Using a Solution 

In make.powerapps.com, switch to your Dev environment. Navigate to Solutions (left menu). 

Click New Solution. Give it a name (e.g., “ALM Training Solution”), choose Publisher (we’ll talk publishers in Part 5; for now use an existing one or create new with your initials), and version (start with 1.0.0.0). Save. 

Now within this solution, add a New Canvas App or New Flow (or add an existing component via Add existing). For example, create a simple Canvas app that shows a hello message; save it inside the solution. 

Also create an Environment Variable in this solution (say, “Environment Name” with a default “Dev”) for practice. 

Try Export Solution (top menu) → choose Unmanaged (since this is Dev) and download the ZIP file. This is your solution package. 

To simulate moving to Test: Import that ZIP into another environment (maybe a Trial environment as your “Test”). Observe that the Canvas app and environment variable appear there after import. 

🍕 Analogy: A solution is a bit like a blueprint binder for a building. The blueprint contains all the architectural components (wiring diagram, plumbing layout, etc.). If you want to replicate the building elsewhere, you hand over the blueprint (solution) – you don’t just verbally describe changes (ad-hoc manual changes risk mistakes). A custom solution binder has just the pages you need, while the Default solution is like an overstuffed binder with everything ever built – not practical to share. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Default Solution vs Unmanaged vs Managed – The exam might present a scenario with someone customizing directly in production (i.e., editing the default solution). That’s incorrect from an ALM perspective. The correct approach is to use an unmanaged solution in Dev, then export as managed to Prod. If a question asks about moving an app to another environment, the answer likely involves exporting a solution package (rather than copying the app manually or re-building it). Also, note that deleting an unmanaged solution does not remove its components (they remain in default solution)176, whereas deleting a managed solution does remove the components (coming in Part 4). This is a frequently tested concept. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Always start your work by creating a custom Solution and building within it. If you have existing apps built outside solutions, migrate them into a solution (Power Apps now allows moving existing apps into solutions via the “Add existing” command). Also, consider using the “Set Preferred Solution” feature – you can mark a custom solution as preferred, ensuring that when makers create new components (even outside a solution context), Power Platform automatically puts them into your preferred solution177. This prevents accidental “orphan” components in the default solution. 

 

Part 4: Managed vs Unmanaged Solutions 

Definitions: 

Unmanaged Solution: A solution in editable form, used in development environments. Unmanaged contains components you can add/remove/modify freely. Think of it as your “source” version178. 

Managed Solution: A solution in deployed form, used in non-dev environments (Test, UAT, Prod). Managed solutions are locked to preserve integrity: you generally cannot directly edit components inside a managed solution179. They are used to install apps without giving away design control. 

🎯 Key Difference: In Dev, you work with Unmanaged solutions; in Prod, you deploy Managed solutions180. Unmanaged = build, Managed = sealed for use. 

Comparison Table – Managed vs. Unmanaged: 

 

Unmanaged Solution 

Managed Solution 

Use Case 

Building/Development. Used actively in Dev environment while making changes181. 

Deployment/Distribution. Used in Test/UAT/Prod to deliver finished features182. 

Editable? 

✅ Yes. Components can be added, removed, modified freely. This is your “working copy”. 

❌ No. Components are read-only (with few exceptions via special actions). Prevents accidental changes in target. 

Exportable? 

✅ Can be exported as unmanaged (for source control, backup) or as managed (to create a managed build artifact)183. 

❌ Cannot be exported from the environment. Once imported as managed, you can’t re-export it. 

Deletion Behavior 

If you delete/uninstall an unmanaged solution, only the solution container is removed; components remain in the environment’s Default solution184. (Effectively, nothing gets deleted—your customizations stick around.) 

If you delete/uninstall a managed solution, all components it brought are removed from the environment185. This includes any data in those components (e.g., a custom table’s data will be deleted)186⚠️. 

Layer 

Exists in the unmanaged layer (top-most layer) in an environment187. All unmanaged solutions share one layer. 

Exists as one or more managed layers. Multiple managed solutions stack; last one imported is on top (can override previous ones)188. 

Dependency on others 

Unmanaged can reference components not in the solution (since environment holds everything). But best practice is to include all needed components. 

Managed solutions formally record dependencies on other solutions. If a managed solution requires another managed solution (say a base solution), you must import that first189. 

Where Used 

Use in development environments only. This is your master copy and also what you check into source control. 

Use in Test/UAT/Production (any environment where you deploy but don’t want changes)190. 

One-Way Street: You build unmanaged, then export as managed. You cannot convert a managed solution back to unmanaged in the target (except by re-importing the original unmanaged or using source control). Also, you cannot import a managed solution into the same environment where its unmanaged version exists (the system will consider that a duplicate)191. So for testing a managed solution, use a different environment. 

🔥 Important Rule: Always treat unmanaged solutions as your “source of truth”192. Keep backups (export unmanaged solutions regularly, or integrate with source control) because if you lose the unmanaged project, a managed alone is not easily editable. Also, only import managed solutions to production – never unmanaged to Prod, to prevent wild, uncontrolled changes by makers in Prod. 

🍕 Analogy: Unmanaged is your “kitchen” copy of a secret recipe – you can tweak ingredients and process while cooking. Managed is the “sealed, delivered dish” served to guests – they can consume it, but they can’t alter the recipe on their plate. If the dish (managed solution) is removed from the table, all its ingredients (components) go away with it. If you drop the recipe card (unmanaged solution), the dish still exists (components remain in the kitchen), though unorganized. 

📍 Practical Exercise: Export and Import Managed vs Unmanaged 

In your Dev environment, open the solution (unmanaged) you created in Part 3. Add a new component (e.g., a new field on a table or a new sample flow) for a change. Publish and Save. 

Export the solution twice: first as Unmanaged (save as Solution1_Unmanaged.zip), and then export again as Managed (Solution1_Managed.zip). 

Prepare two target environments: perhaps use a Trial environment as "Test" and another as "Prod" (or reuse a sandbox from Part 2). 

In the Test environment, Import the Unmanaged solution (like a scenario where someone mistakenly did this in a non-dev environment). Notice after import, you can still edit the components in that Test environment’s solution (not desired in real ALM for Test). 

In the Prod environment, Import the Managed solution. After import, try to edit the components (you shouldn’t be able to – e.g., a form will not allow saving changes). 

Now in Prod environment, Delete (Uninstall) the managed solution. Confirm that the components are completely removed (the app disappears, tables or fields added by it are gone). 

In Test environment, delete the unmanaged solution. Notice that the components are still present in the environment (check in Default solution, the field you added still exists). That’s the unmanaged delete behavior. 

This exercise shows that to cleanly remove something from an environment, it must have been installed as managed. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: A frequent question: “What happens if you delete an unmanaged solution vs a managed solution?” 

Unmanaged: components remain (only solution container is gone)193. 

Managed: components are removed (except data is lost too if you haven’t saved it elsewhere)194. 
Another tricky point: “How do you get a managed solution? Can you create it directly in Prod?” – No, you export unmanaged as managed195. If a question suggests creating a managed solution from scratch or editing managed directly, it’s a trap. 
Also note: “Where should you test a managed solution?” – Not in the same environment as its source; you need a separate target environment196. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Keep one dedicated environment for debugging managed solutions (could be a fresh sandbox). Sometimes you need to simulate the production state (managed) to catch issues that won’t appear in dev (like missing a component in the solution). Don’t forget to version-control or archive your unmanaged solution files. Many companies use a source control repository for the solution (exported as unmanaged, then unpacked with tools for diff). This way, even if a managed solution is deployed, you always have the original source to fix or extend it. 

 

Part 5: Solution Publisher & Prefix 

What is a Solution Publisher? A Publisher in Power Platform identifies the maker or organization behind a solution. Every solution is associated with a publisher, which defines a name and a unique prefix for components created in that solution197. The publisher info travels with the solution and helps manage who “owns” the components. 

Publisher Prefix: The prefix is a short identifier (often 2-5 characters plus underscore) that is prepended to the schema name of new components (like table names, field names, etc.) created in that solution198. For example, if Contoso’s publisher prefix is cts_, a new table “Project” becomes schema name cts_project. This ensures uniqueness and traceability; two different publishers can both create a “Project” table without collisions because their prefixes differ. It’s like a namespace or a “brand” for your components. 

Default Publisher vs Custom Publisher: If you don’t choose a custom publisher, your solution might use the Default Publisher for the environment (with prefix new_)199. Avoid this. The default new_ prefix is generic across all environments, which can cause confusion and collisions. It also signals poor practice – e.g., seeing many fields in a system with the new_ prefix indicates default publisher was used. Always create a custom publisher even for internal projects, with a meaningful prefix (for example, your company or project acronym). This also allows adjusting managed properties later across solutions from the same publisher. 

🔥 Important Rule: Publisher prefix is permanent. Once a component is created, its prefix (and thus association to a publisher) cannot be changed200. Plan your publisher naming carefully upfront, because if you create a bunch of things with dev_ prefix and then want to rename it to contoso_, you’d have to remake those components. A typical convention: use a short form of your company or team name (e.g., “CTSO” or “IT” or “HR”) as prefix. 

How Publisher Affects Solutions: The system uses publisher info in a couple of ways: 

Managed Properties Inheritance: The publisher can define default managed properties for components (like can this component be customized by others). If you trust a certain publisher (like your internal one), you might allow more customization of their components. 

Solution layering considerations: If two solutions from different publishers contain the same component (e.g., both have customizations on Account table), the one installed last (top layer) wins. But if they share the same publisher, some deeper merging or overriding rules apply (like patches or version upgrades can replace earlier layers if same publisher)201. 

📍 Practical Exercise: Create a Custom Publisher 

In make.powerapps.com (Dev environment), go to Solutions -> Publishers (in the command bar, or under solution creation). 

Create a new publisher. Set a friendly Display Name (e.g., “Contoso ALM Publisher”), and a Prefix like cts for Contoso. Save the publisher. 

Now create a new solution using this publisher. Add a new table or field in this solution. After saving the table/field, check its schema name (in advanced settings or by switching to classic). It should start with cts_ (or your chosen prefix), confirming that the publisher prefix is applied. 

(Optional) Try adding an existing component from another solution/publisher. Note that if a component is created under a different publisher originally, it retains its original prefix when included in your solution. That’s why you want to start with the right publisher from the get-go. 

🧠 Memory Trick: Think of the prefix as your solution’s “initials.” Just like authors initial their work, your publisher prefix initials every component you create. Always sign your work! ✍️ 

⚠️ Exam Trap: The exam may ask about default publisher prefix or how to prevent naming collisions. Key fact: default prefix = new_202if you don’t specify another. If an exam question shows components all starting with new_, it’s hinting the default publisher was used – and likely expecting that you point out using a custom publisher is better. Another subtle point: you cannot change a component’s publisher/prefix after creation203; a question might describe someone trying to rename a prefix – they can’t. The remedy is to create a new publisher and move/recreate components under it (which is not trivial). 

💡 Consulting Tip: Standardize on a single publisher (or a small set) for your organization’s solutions. Many ALM issues arise when different projects use different publishers unnecessarily – it complicates dependencies and layering. Have a core “Organization Publisher” for internal work. If multiple teams, consider having a publisher per team but still coordinate prefix naming to avoid overlaps (prefix must be unique per environment). This also helps when you think about “Allowing customization” on managed solutions – often you want to allow only your own organization’s publisher to overlay, not random others. Most Microsoft first-party solutions use their own publisher (e.g., prefix msdyn_ or crm_ for Dynamics). Follow suit with yours. 

 

Part 6: Solution Layers 

What are Solution Layers? Solution layers are the stacked levels of customizations for each component in a Dataverse environment204. When multiple solutions (managed/unmanaged) modify the same component (like a table or form), each solution forms a layer. The system applies a “merge” or “override” logic to determine the final effective behavior for the component. The layering effect ensures you can separate base functionality and customizations. 

Layers and Order: 

System Layer: The default base from Microsoft (out-of-the-box behavior). This is the bottom layer – like the foundation of a house. 

Managed Layers: Every managed solution you import creates a new layer above the previous ones205. The last managed solution imported is the topmost managed layer. If two managed solutions from different publishers change the same thing, the one installed later will take precedence (commonly phrased as “Last one wins” for conflicts)206. 

Unmanaged Layer: All imported unmanaged solutions and direct customizations collectively occupy the single top layer for that component207. There’s effectively one “active” unmanaged layer, which sits above all managed layers in the stack in a dev environment208. Any unmanaged change on a component will override (take precedence) anything from managed layers below it. 

Top Layer Wins vs. Merge Behavior: For most component types, if two layers define the same thing differently, the top layer’s definition is used (override). However, some components (like model-driven app navigation, forms merging sections/tabs, or the application menus) have special merge logic209 210. For example, if two solutions both add fields to a form in different sections, both sets of fields may appear (merged) rather than one completely overwriting the other. But for simpler things (like a field property or a Canvas app’s entire definition), the top layer simply wins. In short: the system tries to merge changes when possible (for certain components); otherwise, the top-most change overrides previous ones211. 

Viewing Solution Layers: Power Apps allows you to inspect layers. For example, in a model-driven app or table form, click Advanced -> See solution layers to view the stack for that component212. You’ll see a list, e.g., “Active (unmanaged)” on top, then layers for each managed solution, down to System at the bottom. 

Unmanaged Customizations Layer: Note that all unmanaged changes share one “Active” layer213. If multiple unmanaged solutions or direct tweaks modify the same component, they effectively co-exist in that single layer. For instance, if you have two unmanaged solutions in Dev environment that both include changes to the Account form, those changes are combined in the one unmanaged layer. (The concept of layering mainly matters when managed solutions come into play, because managed create distinct layers.) 

Removing Layers: If you remove (uninstall) a managed solution, its layer is removed, and the layers below become effective214. For example, if Managed Solution B was overriding something from Managed Solution A, uninstalling B means A’s changes show up again. If you remove an unmanaged customization (like using “Remove Active Customization” or adjusting the component back to default), the layer reverts to showing the managed or base behavior beneath215. 

Analogy – 📚 Stack of Transparencies: Imagine the final application as a stack of transparent sheets. System layer is the original printed sheet. Each managed solution is a transparency with changes – you stack them up. The top sheet’s doodles (changes) show up on the final composite image. Unmanaged layer is like taking a marker and drawing on the topmost transparency itself – it’s the visible top. If you remove that ink (unmanaged changes), you see the next one down (managed layer). If you remove a transparency (uninstall a managed solution), you see the one below it. 

📍 Practical Exercise: Exploring Solution Layers 

Setup: In your Dev environment, create two unmanaged solutions, SolutionA and SolutionB (with the same publisher is fine for now). In SolutionA, add the Account table and customize, say, the form (rename a section or add a field). In SolutionB, also add the Account table’s form and make a different change (like adding a new section). Publish all. Now the Account form has both modifications (since they’re in one unmanaged layer). 

Check Layers in Dev: In Power Apps, open Account form and select See solution layers. You’ll likely see one layer (Active, unmanaged) because both changes coalesced. 

Export SolutionA and SolutionB as managed, version them appropriately (SolutionA v1.0, SolutionB v1.0). 

Import Managed Solutions in test environment: First import SolutionA_managed, then import SolutionB_managed. Now open the Account form in the test environment (read-only). Check solution layers there. You should see SolutionB (managed) on top of SolutionA (managed) on top of System. The form will reflect both changes – either merged or one overriding the other depending on what was done. 

Experiment: Uninstall SolutionB from test environment. Check the form again: now only SolutionA’s changes apply (SolutionB’s layer is gone). Re-import SolutionB, but this time import it before SolutionA (uninstall both, then install B then A). Now A will be on top of B, demonstrating “last imported wins”. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Solution layering and “top wins” logic could appear. Key points: 

Unmanaged layer always on top in Dev, which overrides managed layers216. 

Only certain components (forms, model-driven apps, sitemap) have merge behavior217; most others use top layer. 

If a question describes a scenario with two managed solutions adjusting the same element, answer might revolve around how the last one applied will take effect (top layer). 

Also, “How to allow others to change your managed solution?” – answer: set Managed Properties in the solution (which can allow or prevent overriding by other solutions on top). 

💡 Consulting Tip: Keep solution layers as simple as possible. While layering is powerful, too many layers can make debugging difficult. Prefer a simpler layering model: e.g., one base solution and one overlay solution (like a region-specific adjustments solution). Avoid layering dozens of managed solutions on top of each other for the same components unless absolutely needed. If you need to provide different feature sets to different customers, consider segmented solutions (Part 11) or using feature flags rather than extremely deep layering. Also, always document any known layering, so future maintainers understand which solution controls what. 

 

Part 7: Import, Export, Upgrade, Update, Patch 

When working with solutions, you will export from one environment and import into another, and over time you may release new versions. Power Platform provides multiple options for how to import an updated solution into an environment that already has an earlier version. 

Exporting Solutions: In the maker portal, you can export a solution as either Managed or Unmanaged218. Typically: 

Export Unmanaged (from Dev) to check into source control (so you have a human-readable form after “unpacking” with Solution Packager). 

Export Managed to create the package for importing to Test/Prod (since those should get managed). 

Importing Updated Solutions: When you import a solution that already exists in the target, you see three options for how to handle the update219: 

Upgrade – This is the recommended default for managed solution updates220. It will replace the old solution with the new one entirely, removing any components that were in the old version but not in the new221. Essentially, the target environment will match exactly what’s in the new solution version. Use this when you want a clean update (and you’re okay removing things that got deleted in Dev version). 

Update – This imports the new solution version without removing any components that were removed from the solution in Dev222. Those orphaned components would remain in the target (could cause differences). This is faster but can lead to drift,# Part 1: What is ALM? 
Definition (one-liner): Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) is the end-to-end process of governance, development, and maintenance of software applications through their lifecycles223. It’s about managing your app from conception to planning, building, testing, deployment, and ongoing support in a structured way. 

The 3 Pillars of ALM: 

Governance: Setting up processes and controls (requirements management, security, roles, change tracking, deployment approvals, etc.) to ensure safe, compliant, and repeatable delivery224. 

Application Development: Designing, building, and testing the application (including collaboration between citizen developers & pro developers)225. 

Maintenance/Operations: Deploying and supporting the app in production, including updates, user support, monitoring, and enhancements226. 

ALM Lifecycle Stages: 
ALM is typically visualized as a cyclical process that ensures continuous improvement: Plan & Track → Develop → Build & Test → Deploy → Operate & Monitor → Feedback/Improve227. Each stage flows into the next: you plan features & requirements, develop the app, test it, deploy it to production, operate & monitor its performance, then use feedback to plan enhancements for the next cycle. 

Why ALM matters: Without ALM, things get chaotic. Apps are built in an ad-hoc way, leading to version confusion, broken features, and unpredictable deployments. ALM ensures changes are controlled and traceable, so you avoid the dreaded “It worked in Dev, but it broke Production” scenario. It fosters collaboration between development and operations (DevOps), prevents lost work, and reduces expensive mistakes by making software delivery predictable and repeatable228. 

🍕 Analogy: “ALM is like running a pizza restaurant.” 🍕 You don’t start serving a new pizza recipe to customers without planning ingredients (plan stage), trying out recipes in the kitchen (development), tasting and adjusting the flavor (testing), then deciding it’s ready for the menu (deployment). Skipping ALM steps is like delivering an undercooked pizza – it’s risky and can leave a bad taste! 

🧠 Memory Trick: Think ALM = “All Lifecycle Managed.” It’s about ensuring all stages of an app’s life are managed properly. The key is to remember the cycle: Plan → Build → Test → Deploy → Maintain → Repeat. 

🔥 Important Rule: ALM is not optional for serious projects. Even for low-code solutions, treat them like “real” software with ALM processes for quality and consistency. Good ALM practices will save you from technical debt and deployment disasters229 230. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: ALM is more than just source control or deployment. PL-200 might test your understanding that ALM spans all phases of application management231. For example, you might see a question asking which tasks are part of ALM – the answer includes planning, requirements, testing, deployment, etc., not just writing code. 

 

Part 2: Environment Strategy 

Definition: A Power Platform Environment is a separate, secure container that holds apps, data, flows, connections, and more, typically used to isolate solutions by purpose or stage232. Environments let you manage who has access and separate what runs where (dev, test, production, different departments, etc.), enabling safe ALM processes. 

Environment Types & Comparison: Power Platform provides various environment types, each with distinct use cases and constraints233: 

Environment Type 

Description & Use Case 

Access/Special Notes 

Default 

Auto-created for each tenant; a place for personal apps, exploration, and simple trials. Not meant for production (no manual backup; limited control)234 235.  Use for: personal productivity or learning. 

All licensed users are Environment Makers by default236 (no way to limit creation). Cannot be deleted or manually backed up237. 

Production 

Persistent, full-featured environment for live/mission-critical apps238. Usually long-lived, stable, with formal change controls. Requires available Dataverse capacity.  Use for: Production workloads (live apps, stable config). 

Full admin control, can add security roles, backups enabled. Typically one per solution in production. 

Sandbox 

Non-production environment for developing or testing. Same capabilities as Production but has reset & copy features for safe experimentation239.  Use for: Development, QA testing, or UAT. 

Full control. Admin can convert Production to Sandbox for safe testing (and back)240. Not for long-term use as Prod. 

Developer 

Personal dev environment for one user with Power Apps Developer Plan. A full Dataverse environment just for building & testing individually241.  Use for: Personal development and testing, especially for one-person projects or environment for each developer in a team. 

Limited - owner only by default242 (no sharing via security groups). Remains active as long as user uses it. 

Trial 

Temporary environment for short-term testing or demos. Expires after 30 days (can sometimes extend)243.  Use for: Proof-of-Concepts (PoC) or evaluating features. Not for anything needed long term. 

Full control (like Production) while active. Auto-deletes after expiry. Not for production data. 

(Note: Dataverse for Teams environments also exist for building low-code apps within a Teams context. They have limited capacity and features, and not all ALM capabilities apply, so they are generally beyond this ALM-focused discussion.) 

Basic ALM Environment Pipeline – DEV, TEST, PROD: At minimum, use distinct environments for development and production244. A common ALM strategy is to have at least three environments: Development → Test/UAT → Production245. This ensures you develop and experiment in isolation, test in a safe space replicating production, and deploy to production only after validation. 

1       [DEV Environment] --(after build & test)--> [TEST/UAT Environment] --(after final acceptance)--> [PROD Environment] 

Development (DEV): Where you build and change the solution (unmanaged). Often a sandbox or developer environment dedicated to the project. 

Test/UAT (QA): Where you import the solution as managed and test with users (UAT = User Acceptance Testing). Sometimes multiple test environments are used (QA, UAT, Staging) for complex projects. 

Production (PROD): The live environment used by end users. No development or direct editing should happen here; only import thoroughly tested managed solutions. 

When to Use Sandbox vs Developer vs Production: 

Development work: If a single maker is building a personal app or small changes, using their Developer environment is fine. For team development or larger projects, a shared Sandbox environment (with multiple makers and version control) works well. 

Testing/UAT: Use a Sandbox environment (or even a Production-type environment designated as UAT) to mimic production settings for testing. You can refresh UAT from Prod data if needed, or reset it after testing cycles. 

Live usage: Always use a Production environment for final deployment to end users. 

🔥 Golden Rules for Environment Strategy: 

Never build production apps in the Default environment – it’s not backed up and all users are makers there【1†L60-L66】. Allocate separate production and development spaces. 

Lock down Production: Only specific admins or a dedicated service account should import solutions into Prod. No direct editing of solutions in Prod. 

Align environment versions: Ensure Dev is at same or lower version than Prod, especially if in different regions (Power Platform updates roll out regionally). You can import solutions to newer versions, but not reliably to older versions【3†L43-L47】. So keep development environments updated in sync or behind production’s version. 

Name cleverly: Use clear names (e.g., “Contoso CRM – Dev” / “Contoso CRM – Prod”) to avoid deploying to the wrong environment by accident in pipelines or manually. 

📍 Practical Exercise (Environment Creation): Goal: Create a new environment for development. 

Navigate to the Power Platform Admin Center (admin.powerplatform.microsoft.com). 

Select Environments, then + New. 

Fill in a Name (e.g., “MyProject – Dev Env”), and choose Type = Sandbox (for a dev/test environment). 

Toggle Create a Database if you need Dataverse (for model-driven apps and storing data). Choose a currency and language if prompted. 

Set Security: Add yourself (and your team) as Environment Maker (and an admin if required). 

Click Save. 

Your new environment is ready! It appears in your environment list. Switch to it from the Power Apps or Power Automate maker portal and you can start building solutions there, separate from production. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: The exam may ask about environment types and their intended uses. Remember: Default environment = Everyone’s playground, not for production【1†L60-L67】; Production = long-term environment for real business use; Sandbox = non-prod (dev/test); Trial = short-term disposable. Also, know the minimum ALM pipeline: Dev, (Test), Prod【3†L12-L20】. If a question suggests doing development in the default or production environment, that’s likely a wrong answer (lack of ALM). 

💡 Consulting Tip: In projects, plan your environment strategy early. In enterprise scenarios, consider using Managed Environments (a feature that adds governance controls and extra monitoring to environments). Also, implement Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policies at the environment level to restrict connector usage as needed for compliance. Your environment design and DLP setup are foundational parts of ALM and governance. 

 

Part 3: Solutions — The Container 

Definition: A Solution is a package or container for bundling and moving Power Platform components (apps, flows, entities, etc.) between environments【7†L12-L20】. Solutions are essential for ALM, serving as the “shipping container” for delivering your app customizations in a safe, consistent way【25†L29-L37】. 

What goes inside a Solution: Any custom component you create or modify that is “solution-aware”: 

Apps: Canvas apps and Model-driven apps (the definitions, not the runtime data). 

Power Automate flows: Cloud flows (especially those using Dataverse or connections) should be inside solutions for ALM. 

Dataverse components: Tables & columns, relationships, Choice options (Option Sets), forms, views, business rules, model-driven app site maps, dashboards, etc. 

Chatbots/Agents: Power Virtual Agents bots (including Copilot Studio Agents) belong in solutions for ALM, often with supporting flows and tables. 

Others: Custom connectors, PCF code components, environment variable definitions, AI models, security roles, environment table data like email templates, etc., as needed. 
Essentially, if it’s part of your app’s design or configuration, you want it in a solution for ALM. 

What does not go in a Solution: 

Data (records): Actual business data (rows in Dataverse tables) isn’t included in solution exports【21†L52-L60】. For data migration, you use tools like the Configuration Migration Tool (Part 15) or Power Automate ETL. 

Security roles assignments and user accounts: These are environment-specific. While you can move role definitions as solution components, assigning users to roles is done per environment. 

Connections & secrets: Solutions include Connection References (see Part 9) but not the actual connection credentials – you reconfigure those in each environment. Similarly, Environment Variable current values (like actual API keys, URLs) do not travel with solutions by default【24†L33-L40】【24†L75-L83】. 

Non-solution aware items: Some things like personal (non- solution) flows, canvas apps created outside a solution, or certain app-specific configurations might not be included until you explicitly add them to a solution. 

Default vs. Custom Solutions: 

Default Solution (System) – This is a master solution containing every component in your environment (all apps, tables, flows, etc.). It’s essentially the “global bucket” where changes end up if not added to any other solution【7†L34-L42】. There’s also a “Common Data Service Default Solution” where new items go when you create them outside a named solution. Avoid customizing directly in the default solutions for ALM – that’s like making changes without version control. 

Custom Solution – A user-created solution for your specific project (with a unique name and publisher). Always develop in a custom solution – it isolates your changes, sets a unique prefix, and makes it simple to export just those changes. Microsoft strongly recommends not working in the default solution directly【7†L36-L44】. 

Why always use custom solutions? 

Isolation: Only include relevant components. The default solution holds everything, which is messy. Custom solutions let you package only what your project needs (making deployment and troubleshooting easier). 

Prefix control: With a custom solution, you define a solution Publisher and Prefix (see Part 5) that uniquely tags your components (e.g., abc_Account vs out-of-the-box new_Account). This avoids naming collisions and establishes ownership【8†L75-L83】. 

Source Control: You can export an unmanaged solution and check it into Git or Azure DevOps easily, which is crucial for collaborative development and backups. 

🎯 Key Concept: Solutions = ALM packaging【7†L12-L20】. You can think of a solution file (a .zip file when exported) as a snapshot of your app’s design – it can be moved to another environment, imported, and published to replicate the app there. This is how you implement ALM: by moving solutions between Dev/Test/Prod with the necessary components. 

🍕 Analogy: “A solution is a moving box 📦 for your app.” When moving houses, you wouldn’t carry all your clothes and dishes individually; you pack them into boxes so nothing gets lost in transit. Similarly, pack your app components into a solution for moving them around safely. Also, like labeling a box with its contents, solutions have a Name and Version so you know what’s inside and its release level. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Create a Solution: 

In your Dev environment (say on make.powerapps.com), click Solutions in the left nav. 

Click + New solution. Give it a clear Display Name (e.g., “TimeOffApp Solution”). Notice a Publisher is required – select an existing one or create a new (we’ll discuss in Part 5). The Version typically starts at 1.0.0.0 (you can leave it). 

Click Create. You now have a blank container. 

Add existing components or create new ones inside this solution. For example, within the solution, click New → select Table, App, Flow, etc. or Add existing to bring in an already-created component. 

After making some components, try exporting the solution: Select your solution → Export → choose Unmanaged for now, and download the ZIP. Open it to see the XML files – these describe your components. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Be ready for questions on what’s included in a solution export vs what isn’t. Data (records) never moves in a solution【21†L52-L60】 – that requires separate tooling. Also, connection references and environment variables in solutions do not include secrets or actual values – the exam may test that you need to reconfigure connections and env variable values after import. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Modularize your solutions. For large projects, consider splitting into multiple smaller solutions (e.g., core vs optional features, or per feature module). This can make team development and versioning easier, and reduce risk that one solution’s changes might accidentally affect unrelated parts of the system. However, be mindful of cross-solution dependencies (see Part 12). 

 

Part 4: Managed vs Unmanaged Solutions 

Definitions: In Power Platform ALM, Unmanaged solutions are for building (the editable, raw state), whereas Managed solutions are for deploying (locked-down packages)【8†L20-L28】. 

Unmanaged Solutions (“Development mode”): 

Editable: You can add, remove, or modify components freely in an unmanaged solution. This is the mode used in development environments【8†L24-L31】. 

Source of Truth: Unmanaged is considered the “source” of your work. You typically check the unmanaged solution (or its extracted files) into source control【8†L22-L27】. 

Export Options: You can export an unmanaged solution either as unmanaged (for backup or moving to another dev environment) or as managed (for deployment)【8†L22-L27】. 

Deletion Behavior: If you delete (remove) an unmanaged solution from an environment, the components remain in the environment. Only the solution container is removed【8†L24-L31】. The components are still present, just now under the default solution. This is why unmanaged solutions aren’t meant to be uninstalled in the same way – they’re just a working container. 

Managed Solutions (“Deployment mode”): 

Locked & Sealed: A managed solution is a finalized package (created by exporting an unmanaged solution as managed) for import into Test/UAT/Prod. Components within it cannot be directly modified in the target (only via layering/unmanaged customizations)【8†L24-L31】. 

Not Editable: You cannot edit a managed solution’s components unless you “unmanage” them by adding your own layer on top or using special tools. The whole point is to treat it like a sealed app. 

Must be exported from an unmanaged: You cannot create a managed solution from scratch. You always develop in an unmanaged solution and then export it as managed to get the managed form for distribution【8†L24-L31】. 

Deletion Behavior: Deleting (uninstalling) a managed solution from an environment will remove its components entirely (and potentially any data related to them)【8†L24-L31】【8†L29-L35】. For example, uninstalling a managed solution that introduced a custom table will delete that table and all data in it (unless backed up). This ensures no “artifact leftovers.” 

Comparison Table: Managed vs. Unmanaged 

Aspect 

Unmanaged Solution 

Managed Solution 

Use in ALM 

Development (Dev env) – Work in progress.【8†L24-L31】 

Deployment (Test/Prod env) – Finished package.【8†L24-L31】 

Editability 

Yes – Fully editable by makers. 

No – Components locked (need to add to an unmanaged solution or have proper permission to change)【8†L24-L31】. 

Export Options 

Can export as unmanaged or managed. 

Cannot export a managed solution (once imported as managed)【8†L24-L31】. 

Deletion/Uninstall 

Deleting solution keeps components (they remain in default solution)【8†L24-L31】. 

Uninstalling removes all components from environment【8†L24-L31】【8†L29-L35】 (deletes data in custom tables). 

Suitable for 

Iterative building, source control, moving between Dev environments. 

Clean deployment to Test/Prod, versioned releases, third-party distribution. 

Dependencies 

Can reference managed comps; minimal enforcement. 

Enforces dependencies (cannot import if prerequisites missing; cannot remove if dependencies exist)【8†L88-L96】. 

How Managed & Unmanaged Work Together: In an ALM pipeline, developers build an unmanaged solution in Dev, then export it as a managed solution to deploy to Test/Prod【8†L24-L31】. You typically keep the unmanaged version for source control and future development, and treat the managed version as a build artifact for release【8†L24-L31】. 

🔥 Important Rule: Never edit production (managed) components directly. If something in Prod (managed) needs changing, go back to Dev, adjust the unmanaged solution, then re-export a new managed version for Prod. Don’t be tempted to “quick fix” in production by adding an unmanaged customization there – it creates a messy layer and dependency that later blocks uninstalling the solution【8†L24-L31】. The exam might call this scenario “a dependency that prevents a managed solution uninstall.” 

🍕 Analogy: “Unmanaged vs Managed is like working with an original vs laminated document.” 📄 → 🗄️ When you’re writing an important document, you work on a draft that you can edit (like an unmanaged solution). Once finalized, you laminate it to make it permanent and tamper-proof (like a managed solution). If you need to change something on the laminated copy, you must go back to the original document (the unmanaged version), edit it, and then laminate a new copy (export a new managed version). 

📍 Practical Exercise – Export & Import (and observe Delete Behavior): 

In Dev environment, open your unmanaged solution from Part 3. Add a component (e.g., create a new custom table or field). 

Export the solution: Select Export → choose Unmanaged first (this is your backup/source). Then export again, but choose Managed. Now you have two files: e.g., Solution_1_0_0_0_managed.zip and Solution_1_0_0_0_unmanaged.zip. 

Switch to your Test environment. Import the Managed solution (_managed.zip). Publish if not auto-published. Confirm the new component is present, but try editing it – it should be locked or restricted (managed state). 

Now delete (uninstall) the managed solution from Test: In Solutions, select it and click Delete. After deletion, check for the component you added (e.g., look for that custom table or field). It should be gone 🏃, because removing the managed solution removed all its components. 

For contrast, if you import the Unmanaged solution into another Dev environment (if available) and then delete the solution record, the new component will still remain in that environment’s default solution (since uninstalling unmanaged doesn’t remove components). 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Unmanaged vs Managed often appears on PL-200. Key things to remember: 

Unmanaged is for dev, and can be exported as managed for other envs【8†L24-L31】. 

You cannot directly import a managed solution back into the original development environment where the unmanaged version exists【8†L27-L32】 (doing so would cause conflicts – exam might ask what to do if you want to test a managed solution; answer: use a separate test environment). 

If a solution’s question involves making edits in test or prod, answer likely involves unmanaged customization layer or bringing the item into an unmanaged solution first – direct edit of managed components is not allowed【8†L24-L31】. 

 

Part 5: Solution Publisher & Prefix 

What is a Solution Publisher? A Publisher in Power Platform identifies the creator/owner of a solution, and it defines the prefix that stamps every component (like table, field schema names) created within that solution【8†L62-L70】【8†L73-L81】. 

The Role of Publisher & Prefix: 

Publisher Name: A friendly name (e.g., “Contoso” or “XYZ Solutions”). Primarily informational, showing who the solution belongs to. 

Publisher Prefix: A short identifier, typically 2-4 letters, that is prepended to custom item schema names. For example, if your prefix is contoso, a new table might be named contoso_MyNewTable, and a field might be contoso_Field1. This ensures that solution components have unique logical names to avoid conflicts【8†L75-L83】. 

Why not use the Default Publisher? 
If you don’t specify, new components get a default prefix new_ (the default publisher). Avoid using the default new_ prefix – it’s generic and could clash with other solutions. Also, it’s considered poor ALM practice as it doesn’t identify who built the component. Creating a custom publisher ensures your components are uniquely labeled and traceable to your team or project【8†L75-L83】. 

Prefix is Permanent: Choose your prefix wisely upfront. Once components exist with a prefix, you cannot change their prefixes later【8†L75-L83】. For instance, if abc_ is your prefix, all your component names (tables, fields, etc.) will start with abc_, forever. To change it, you’d have to recreate components under a different publisher – not trivial. So plan a meaningful, likely unique prefix. Common patterns: company abbreviations or project code (e.g., CO for Contoso or HRIS for HR system). 

Example Best Practices: 

Use uppercase or short prefixes (2-5 characters) that identify your org or project (like CTSO, HR, PAY, etc). 

Avoid ambiguous letters or very common combos. 

Make sure no two publishers in your organization use the same prefix to avoid confusion or collisions. 

🔥 Important Rule: Always create a custom publisher (with a unique prefix) and use it for all custom solutions. This ensures proper ownership and no naming clashes. If you see new_ on something in prod, it likely means someone used the default publisher – a sign of poor ALM hygiene. 

🍕 Analogy: “A Publisher prefix is like an artist’s signature 🎨 on a painting.” It identifies who made it. Imagine a gallery where every painting is signed “Anonymous” – you’d never track which artist did what. The default new_ prefix is like “Anonymous,” whereas your custom prefix is your unique signature on each component, helpful for long-term management. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Creating a Custom Publisher: 

Open make.powerapps.com in your Dev environment, go to Solutions. 

Click + New publisher. Provide a Name (“Contoso Ltd” or your name/team), Unique Name (auto-filled), and a Prefix (for example, cts for Contoso)【8†L75-L83】. You can also set a contact (an email or website) for this publisher. 

Save the publisher. Now you can create a new solution and select this Publisher. Any new component you create in this solution will automatically get the prefix (e.g., cts_) in its schema name. 

Test it: Within your new solution (with your publisher), create a new table and a new column. Go to Advanced settings or classic solution explorer to see the schema name of these items – they should start with your prefix (e.g., cts_). 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Remember that prefixes ensure uniqueness at the schema level – not UI display names but the underlying logical names (like schema_name for a field). Also, know that the default publisher’s prefix is “new”【7†L36-L40】 and that using it is discouraged. If the exam asks “how to ensure custom fields from different solutions don’t conflict?”, the answer revolves around custom publishers and prefixes. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Many organizations define a single publisher for all their solutions (e.g., OrgShortName as prefix). This ensures all custom components across solutions share a common prefix, simplifying environment management. However, if you have multiple teams, you might give each team or project its own prefix. The key is to keep a registry so no duplicates. And again: decide early – it’s painful to change later! 

 

Part 6: Solution Layers 

What are Solution Layers? Solution layers are the stacked order of customizations from different solutions in an environment, which determine the final behavior of components【10†L6-L14】. Each component (e.g., a form, field, flow) can have multiple layers from different solutions; the topmost layer “wins” for that component’s runtime behavior【10†L27-L35】. 

Layer Order (Highest to Lowest): 

Unmanaged Customizations layer (top) – If any unmanaged changes exist for a component, they override all managed layers below【10†L6-L14】. All unmanaged solutions share one combined layer, since at runtime they are treated collectively as “the active state” of the environment【10†L6-L14】. 

Managed layers (middle) – Each imported managed solution adds a layer above any previously installed managed solutions【10†L8-L16】. The most recently imported managed solution’s changes sit on top of older ones. If two managed solutions change the same component, the one imported last (higher layer) wins unless a special merge rule applies【10†L27-L35】. 

Base System layer (bottom) – The default built-in system behavior of Dataverse, plus the Original (unmanaged) definitions of any components that were first introduced, form the foundation. If no other layers exist above, the system layer defines the component’s behavior. 

1        [ Unmanaged Customizations (top) ]   <-- Active dev changes here override below  

2        [ Managed Solution B (latest)     ]   <-- Last imported managed solution’s changes 

3        [ Managed Solution A (earlier)    ]   <-- First imported managed solution’s changes 

4        [ System (out-of-box base)       ]   <-- Original platform defaults or base components 

Top-Layer Wins vs Merge Behavior: By default, most components use a “top-layer wins” rule, meaning the highest layer’s definition completely overrides any lower layers【10†L31-L39】. However, a few component types merge instead of override: notably model-driven app definitions, forms, and site maps can merge contributions from multiple solutions (e.g., two solutions adding fields to the same form will result in a form with both fields)【10†L27-L35】. The goal is to preserve functionality, sometimes at the cost of layout changes if needed to resolve conflicts【10†L49-L57】. 

Example: If both Solution A and Solution B (imported later) modify Form X, the system will attempt to merge changes (if possible) to include elements from both. But if both solutions modify a field’s name, only the top layer’s change shows (“top wins”). 

When you uninstall a top managed solution layer, the layer below it (another managed solution or base) is revealed and becomes active【10†L8-L16】. For instance, if managed Solution B made a change to a form, uninstalling it will revert that form to how managed Solution A (or the base) defined it. 

Viewing Solution Layers: In the Power Apps maker portal, you can inspect layers. Go to Solutions → Default Solution, find a component (like a column or form) and click See solution layers (in classic, “Show Dependencies” also shows layers for that component)【7†L82-L90】. This will list the layers present and their order (unmanaged at top if present, then managed layers in descending import order, then system at bottom). 

Unmanaged Customizations Layer: This is essentially a single combined layer that holds all active unmanaged changes for a component【10†L6-L14】. If you have multiple unmanaged solutions, they don’t stack separately – any change from any unmanaged solution (or direct customization) goes into this single top layer. That’s why it’s okay to have multiple unmanaged solutions editing the same component; they all contribute to one layer in runtime. But it’s also why direct “hotfix” changes in Production (unmanaged customizations on top of managed) can complicate things – they block removal of the underlying managed solution until removed (because they are dependent on it). 

Removing Layers: Removing (uninstalling) a managed solution removes its layer. If it’s the top layer for a component, the next layer’s settings take effect【10†L8-L16】. If you remove an unmanaged customization (via the UI’s “Remove Active Customization” option in classic solution explorer), you essentially clear the unmanaged layer, revealing the managed or base definitions below【12†L57-L60】. 

🔥 Important Rule: The top-most layer always defines what end-users see or experience (except merging cases). So always know what’s on top. For example, if an urgent Prod fix is done via unmanaged customization on top of a managed solution, that will mask any underlying managed changes for that component. Use the  “Solution Layers” view to verify what’s applied. 

🍕 Analogy: “Solution layers are like layers in a photo editing app 📷.” The bottom layer is the original image (system). Managed solution layers are like adding semi-transparent overlays or new elements (stickers) on the image; the last sticker you put is visible on top. Unmanaged customizations are like drawing on the topmost layer – covering everything beneath. If you peel off (remove) the top sticker (uninstall top solution), you see what’s underneath again. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Inspect Solution Layers: 

In a Dev environment, create a new table in a custom unmanaged solution (Solution A). Set some property (e.g., set Display Name to “Customer” and a specific Color icon). Export as managed and import into a Test environment. 

In Dev, change the table’s Display Name to “Client” (modify Solution A) and export as a new version managed (v2). Import into Test – now the table is “Client”. 

Still in Test, use the classic Solution Explorer to view the table’s layers. You should see System (with original name), then Solution A v1, then Solution A v2 on top. The top layer shows the current name “Client”. 

Now remove the top managed solution (Solution A v2) from Test. After it’s gone, refresh the table list – the table name might revert to “Customer” (the v1 layer now on top). 

Remove the first solution (the last managed layer). The table will be gone (since the base System had no such table). 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Be prepared to answer questions on “last one wins” vs “merge” behavior【10†L27-L35】. If asked “which components support merging of layers?” – think model-driven app components (like forms, site map, app navigation). Everything else is typically “top layer wins”. Also know that multiple unmanaged changes form one layer – an exam question might be: “You have two unmanaged solutions editing a form. Which one’s changes apply?” Answer: Both changes are combined in the single unmanaged layer. 

 

Part 7: Import, Export, Upgrade, Update, Patch 

When moving solutions between environments, you have options on how to export and import. Export can produce a managed or unmanaged solution file (as we saw). On importing a solution into an environment where that solution (or an earlier version) already exists, the system offers options: Upgrade, Stage for Upgrade, or Update【20†L23-L31】. There are also solution patches to consider. Here’s the definition and differences: 

Exporting Solutions: 

As Unmanaged: Usually for moving your solution between development environments or for source control. Keeps it editable (unmanaged). 

As Managed: For deploying to Test/UAT/Prod or sharing with others as final product. Seals it (non-editable). You cannot open a managed solution file to see raw code or re-export it once imported – it’s meant for consumption only【8†L24-L31】. 

Import Options (Upgrade vs Update vs Stage for Upgrade): 

Upgrade (formerly “Stage + Apply” in one step): This is the recommended way to import a newer version of a managed solution over an older version. It replaces the old version completely – any components removed in the new version get deleted from the environment【20†L23-L30】. It’s essentially a “full replace” ensuring the environment matches exactly the new solution’s content. Use this when you want to cleanly update and remove what’s no longer needed. 

Stage for Upgrade: This is a two-step approach. First, when you import with “Stage for Upgrade,” the system imports the new version alongside the old, marking the new one as a temporary “_Upgrade” solution layer【20†L23-L31】. At this point, both versions exist, with the new one on top (so it’s active). This allows you to do any additional manual data migration or verification while still retaining the old solution. Then you must explicitly click “Apply Upgrade” to remove the old version and finalize the upgrade【20†L31-L39】. If you don’t apply, both versions sit there, which isn’t meant to be long-term. 

Update: This option imports the new solution version but does not delete any removed components from the previous version【20†L23-L31】. It essentially overwrites existing components with new ones, but if something was removed in the new version, it stays as an unused component in the environment. This is faster and sometimes safer if you’re not sure about deleting anything yet, but over successive updates, your environment might accumulate “orphaned” components. Use update when you only added new components or changed existing ones without wanting to remove old parts yet. 

Comparison: Upgrade vs Update vs Stage for Upgrade 

Criteria 

Upgrade (Single-step) 

Stage for Upgrade (Two-step) 

Update (No delete) 

Removes components not in new version? 

✅ Yes (immediately deletes removed components)【20†L24-L31】 

🔴 Not immediately (holds off deletion until manual “Apply Upgrade”)【20†L23-L31】【20†L31-L39】 

❌ No (keeps removed components; they remain in the system)【20†L23-L30】. 

Process 

One import action. Old version and patches rolled up and replaced in one go【20†L23-L31】. 

Import new version (now two versions exist) → separate step to “Apply Upgrade” which then removes old version【20†L23-L31】【20†L31-L39】. 

One import action, simply merges new changes on top of existing. 

Performance 

Medium – does removal as part of import. 

Slowest – two steps, and temporarily have duplicate components. 

Fastest import – minimal changes. 

When to use 

Preferred for clean alignment with source version (especially if components were deleted or renamed) to avoid clutter. 

When you need to run parallel for a moment (maybe to migrate data or verify new version) before removing old version. 

For quick minor updates that only add or change, no deletions (ensures nothing in environment is lost). 

Patches (Solution Patch): A patch is a specialized solution that contains only the changes since a parent solution version【8†L55-L63】. The idea was to deliver small hotfixes without full solution upgrades. Patches increment the version’s build number (third segment) and can be rolled up into a higher version later【8†L53-L61】. However, Microsoft now generally recommends focusing on full solution upgrades instead of patches【10†L19-L22】. Patches can complicate layering and are mostly considered a legacy approach. Instead, just create a new solution version for your fixes and do an upgrade. 

“Holding” Solutions (Clone & Patch, Clone & Update): In older approaches, makers might “clone a solution to a patch” or “clone solution to a new version” using classic solution explorer. Cloning a patch created a new patch with a version like 1.0.1.0, small changes could be made, and eventually you’d “roll up” into a 1.1.0.0. Cloning to new version essentially created a new higher version from existing. These actions have been largely replaced by improved modern Update/Upgrade flows in the UI. Patches remain supported but are less emphasized now【10†L19-L22】. 

Practical Sequence – Upgrading Solutions: 

In Dev, increment your solution version before export (e.g., from 1.0.0.0 to 1.1.0.0 or 1.0.1.0 depending on significance). Do this by editing the version number in solution settings before exporting. 

Export the new version as Managed. 

In Test/Prod, when importing, choose Upgrade (or Stage for Upgrade if you need to perform extra actions before completing). If you choose Update, be aware orphaned components won’t be removed. 

If Stage for Upgrade was used, don’t forget to later click Apply Upgrade to finish the process. Confirm in solution list that the older version is gone after applying. 

⚠️ Exam Traps: 

Understand what patches can’t do: Patches cannot remove components, only add/modify【8†L57-L61】. If an exam question involves deleting a component via ALM, the answer likely involves doing a solution upgrade (not a patch) or removing the component and using the upgrade approach to delete it in target【20†L23-L31】【20†L39-L47】. 

Recognize the effect of each import option: e.g., if you need to ensure removed components from previous versions are deleted in target, choose Upgrade. If you want to keep them, choose Update【20†L23-L31】. A common trap is a scenario where something remains in the target environment because the user did an Update instead of Upgrade. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Have a versioning strategy (see Part 16). Always update the version before exporting a solution for release – it’s easier to track what’s where. Use  Upgrade for major releases or any change involving removals; use Update for minor or urgent changes when you can’t risk deleting anything yet (but plan to do a cleanup upgrade later). Patches might be useful in very specific cases (like emergency fix to a live solution version without revving the main solution), but prefer simply incrementing version and doing an upgrade for clarity. 

 

Part 8: Environment Variables 

What are Environment Variables? Environment Variables (EVs) are configurable parameters that can be used by apps, flows, etc., to adjust behavior per environment【24†L14-L23】. They enable you to separate hard-coded values (like URLs, IDs, or environment-specific settings) from your solution’s logic, making it easier to adapt when moving between Dev, Test, Prod. 

Why Environment Variables matter: Suppose your app calls a REST API with a URL that’s different in Dev vs Prod. Instead of editing the flow/app after each deployment (error-prone), you use an Environment Variable for the URL. In each environment, you set a different value. The app references the EV, so it automatically points to the correct URL in each environment【24†L14-L23】. This is crucial for ALM because it allows the solution to be the same, while external configurations differ. 

Default Value vs Current Value: Every environment variable has two value slots【24†L35-L43】: 

Default Value (in definition): The optional baseline value, stored in the solution definition. This travels with the solution as part of the Environment Variable Definition component【24†L35-L43】. 

Current Value: A value set per environment after import, which overrides the default. This does not travel with the solution – it lives in each environment’s database【24†L35-L43】. If a current value exists in an environment, it takes precedence over the default at runtime. If not, the default (if provided) is used【24†L35-L43】. 
Practical point: Usually, you include an EV with a default placeholder (or no default) in your solution, then after importing, set the Current Value in the target environment (like providing the actual URL, API key, etc. for that environment)【24†L75-L83】【24†L85-L90】. 

Types of Environment Variables: Common types include Text, Number, Yes/No (Two options), JSON (for structured configs), Data Source, and Secret【24†L35-L43】. 

Text/Number/Yes or No: simple values for strings, numeric, booleans. 

JSON: store more complex structured info (like a JSON config). 

Data Source: references a data connection or specific environment resource (e.g., a SharePoint site URL or Dataverse table ID), often created automatically when adding data sources in canvas apps【24†L39-L47】【24†L51-L58】. 

Secret: used with Azure Key Vault – you provide a Key Vault reference so that sensitive values (like API keys) aren’t stored in plain text in Dataverse, but fetched from a Key Vault at runtime【24†L37-L45】. 

How they travel in Solutions: Environment variable definitions are solution components, so you add them to your solution. The Definition (with default value) moves with the solution, but the current values do not (unless you deliberately included them – and typically you shouldn’t)【24†L35-L43】. The idea is to set the appropriate current values in each environment after import. 

When importing a managed solution, the UI will prompt you to enter environment variable values if the solution has EV definitions without a value set yet in the target【24†L75-L83】. This ensures you don’t forget them. 

🧠 Memory Trick: “Default vs Current = Template vs Actual.” The Default Value is like a template or suggestion (maybe dev’s value), while the Current Value is the actual setting in this environment. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Using Environment Variables: 

In a Dev environment, within a solution, create a new Environment Variable (New → More → Environment Variable). Set Display Name (“API Base URL”), Data Type = Text, Default Value = https://dev-api.contoso.com. Save it【24†L33-L41】. 

In a flow or canvas app, reference this Environment Variable (for flows, you can add the EV to the trigger or action parameters via the dynamic content named “Environment Variables”). 

Export the solution as a managed solution and import it into a Test environment. During import, you’ll see a prompt to set the environment variable value【24†L75-L83】. Provide a Test-specific URL (e.g., https://test-api.contoso.com). 

After import in Test, verify that the environment variable’s Current Value is set to the test URL, while Default Value still shows the dev URL. The flow/app in Test now uses the Test URL automatically. 

Try exporting the managed solution from Test (if you could) – you’d see only the default value persists; the current value https://test-api... stays in Test and is not part of the solution package. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Ensure you know where environment variable values are stored. The exam could ask: “You move a solution with an environment variable to a new environment. What must you do for it to work?” The answer: Set the environment variable’s current value in the target environment (since default might not be relevant, and current value doesn’t carry over). Also remember: environment variable values can be edited in the target environment without unlocking the solution because the value is not considered a customization of the solution – it’s stored separately. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Use Environment Variables for any configuration that changes per environment (URLs, SharePoint site IDs, file paths, feature flags). Don’t hardcode environment-specific constants in apps or flows. This will save you headaches when promoting solutions. For secrets (like API keys, DB connection strings), use Azure Key Vault with EV of type Secret. This way, the actual secret stays in Key Vault and only a reference ID is in the solution settings – improving security. Always document what environment variables need setting after import (like in a release note or README for your solution). 

 

Part 9: Connection References 

What are Connection References? A Connection Reference is a solution component that acts as a placeholder for a connection to an external service, used by things like flows or canvas apps【25†L39-L47】. It allows you to configure the actual connection separately in each environment without changing the flow/app directly. 

Why Connection References exist: Many Power Apps and Power Automate flows use connectors (to SharePoint, Outlook, etc.). The actual Connections (like “Contoso SharePoint connection using Alice’s credentials”) are environment-specific and user-specific, and cannot be exported in a solution. Instead, a connection reference is like a tag or alias inside your solution that says, “this flow uses a SharePoint connection with a certain name.” When you import the solution, you then “map” that connection reference to an actual Connection in the target environment【13†L33-L40】. 

How to work with Connection References: 

When you build a flow or canvas app inside a solution, each time you use a connector (like SharePoint, Outlook, SQL etc.), the system will prompt you to select an existing connection or create a new one; it then automatically creates a connection reference in the solution referencing that connection**【25†L39-L47】. 

The connection reference is stored by a unique name/ID and connector type. It doesn’t include sensitive data. 

On importing the solution to another environment, you must have a matching connection (to the same service) and you’ll be prompted to map each connection reference to a specific connection in the target environment. For example, if your solution has a “SharePoint connection reference” expecting a SharePoint connection, you must pick which existing SharePoint connection (or create a new one) in the target environment to bind it to. 

Once mapped, the flows/apps in the solution will use that mapped connection (all actions within them that used the connection reference now use the actual connection). 

🧠 Memory Trick: “Connection Reference = key to plug in a connection.” Just as you use a key to start a car, a connection reference is like the keyhole; you still need to provide a key (actual connection details) in each environment. 

Difference: Connection vs Connection Reference: 

A Connection is the actual authenticated link to a service (e.g., a login to SharePoint or a specific SQL connection string stored in the environment). It’s a resource not tied to a solution. 

A Connection Reference is a pointer within a solution that says “this solution’s components need a connection of type X.” 
Think of the reference as the slot, and the connection as the plug that fits into it. You pack the slot with your solution, and when you import it, you plug in the actual connection in that environment. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Using Connection References: 

In your Dev environment, within a solution, create a New flow (e.g., an Instant cloud flow). In a step, add an action from a connector like SharePoint. Notice you must choose a Connection (like a specific SharePoint connection profile). After saving, check the solution’s components list – you’ll see a Connection Reference added automatically (with a name like “SharePoint Connection”). 

Export the solution as managed and import to Test/Prod. During import, the wizard will show a step to review connections. It will likely indicate this connection reference needs mapping. Select a corresponding Connection (e.g., create a new one to your Test SharePoint site or pick an existing). Complete the import. 

Open the flow in the new environment – confirm it’s using the connection you mapped. The connection reference is automatically bound to that environment’s connection. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: If asked, “How do you include a Power Automate flow in a solution for ALM?”, remember: you need to use Connection References for any connectors the flow uses (the exam expects you to know flows must be in solutions to take advantage of ALM features like connection refs). Also remember that Connection references allow flows and canvas apps to connect to environment-specific services after import without modifying the app/flow design – you just set up the connection mapping. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Keep connection references generic. For example, if a flow uses a SharePoint list named “ProjectDocs” which will vary by environment (Dev vs Prod site), consider also using an Environment Variable for the site URL. The connection reference handles which credential and connector instance to use; the environment variable can supply environment-specific parameters (like site URL, list name, etc.). This reduces manual editing after import. Also, regularly audit connection references – e.g., if two solutions use the same connectors with similar purpose, you might reuse one connection reference in both or ensure naming clarity to avoid confusion. 

 

Part 10: Solution Checker (Quality Gate) 

What is Solution Checker? The Power Platform Solution Checker is a built-in static analysis tool that scans your solutions for potential issues across performance, stability, security, reliability, and best practices【7†L51-L59】【7†L67-L71】. It provides a report of findings, from critical “must-fix” issues to suggested improvements. 

What it checks: Solution Checker inspects your canvas apps, model-driven apps, flows, and code (like plug-ins or PCF) for known issues: 

Performance: e.g., inefficient formulas in canvas apps, poorly performing queries, etc. 

Maintainability & Design: e.g., overly complex expressions, missing error-handling in flows, deprecated connectors usage. 

Best Practices & Supportability: e.g., usage of experimental features, naming conventions, accessibility issues, or API calls that might break in future. 

Security: e.g., potential vulnerabilities in code components, or things like flows that allow privilege escalation. 

Severity Levels: The findings are typically categorized as Critical, High, Medium, Low, or Informational, indicating priority. Critical/High issues should be addressed before production to avoid failures or performance problems, whereas Lower ones are often suggestions for improvement or future fix. 

When & How to run it: Run Solution Checker early and often – ideally before exporting a solution for testing or release. In the modern Solution view, simply select your solution and click “Solution checker” → Run【7†L67-L71】. It will run in the background and then you can view the results in the Power Apps portal (and download as Excel or PDF if needed). 

You can also integrate it into pipelines using Power Platform CLI or Azure DevOps tasks, making it an automatic quality gate (fail a build if critical issues are found) – a great practice for enterprise ALM. 

Common issues detected: 

For canvas apps: complicated formulas that should be simplified, controls set to deprecated properties, missing accessibility labels. 

For flows: usage of “Apply to each” with too many actions, or missing error handling in parallel branches. 

Performance issues like retrieving too many records without delegation (in Power Apps), or missing indexes on large tables. 

Code issues (if you have code components or plug-ins) like poor patterns or security concerns. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Running Solution Checker: 

In make.powerapps.com, after building a solution (with at least some app or flow), click Solutions, select your solution. 

Click Solution checker in the command bar, then Run【7†L67-L71】. The scan might take a few minutes. 

Once complete, click Solution checker again and select View Results. Examine the issues listed. Click on a finding to see details and guidance. 

Try fixing one of the reported issues (if any). For example, if a Canvas app formula warning appears, go edit the app and refine the formula. Then save, publish the app, and re-run the checker to see if the issue is resolved. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: The exam might include a question on the purpose of Solution Checker (e.g., ensuring quality by identifying issues like performance or security concerns before deployment). Remember that it’s integrated in the solution UI and can also be used in pipelines. Also, it covers not just code but no-code components (like flows and canvas apps), so don’t think it’s only for developers – it’s for everyone to improve solution quality. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Treat Solution Checker as a “QA reviewer” that never gets tired. Enforce that solution checker passes with no critical or high issues before a solution moves to UAT or Prod. Consider making it part of your CI/CD pipeline using the PowerApps checker PowerShell or Azure DevOps extension for continuous quality enforcement. This fosters a quality-first culture among makers, catching issues early when they’re cheaper to fix. 

 

Part 11: Segmented Solutions 

What are Segmented Solutions? Segmented Solutions refer to packaging only selected components (or parts of components) in your solution, rather than entire modules, to reduce dependencies and size【11†L12-L21】. It’s also known as table segmentation for solutions. 

Why and When to Use Segmentation: In large implementations, sometimes you don’t want to include everything in one monolithic solution, especially when doing incremental updates. Segmentation allows you to include only the updated pieces, reducing the risk of overwriting other customizations and speeding up import times【11†L12-L18】【11†L29-L37】. For instance, if you updated just one form on the Account table, you might export a solution containing only that updated form, not the entire Account table with all forms, views, fields. This technique is helpful for big projects where multiple teams work on different parts of the system, or for applying hotfixes that touch few components. 

How to Create a Segmented Solution: When adding existing components (especially Dataverse tables) to a solution, the interface gives options to pick specific sub-components【11†L14-L22】: 

No objects selected: Adds only minimal table info (just an empty stub); no subcomponents like columns or forms. 

Edit objects: Manually select specific subcomponents (individual columns, forms, views, etc.) to include【11†L14-L22】. This is a key to segmentation – pick only what changed. 

Include table metadata: Adds all table settings (like auditing, etc.) but no subcomponents. 

Include all objects: Adds the table and everything related (all columns, all forms, etc.)【11†L14-L22】. Use this only when introducing a brand new table that doesn’t exist in the target environment yet【11†L19-L25】 (so no risk of overwriting something pre-existing) – because it brings the entire entity over. 

Benefits and Caution: 

Pros: Smaller solution files, faster import/exports, lower risk of overriding something unintentionally because you’re only moving what you intended to change【11†L29-L37】. Helps in multi-team environments to isolate changes for specific areas. 

Cons: Requires careful planning to include all necessary pieces. If you miss a dependency, import will error (e.g., forgetting that a new field on a form also needs to be in the solution). Also, can lead to many separate solution files to manage if overused. 

Example scenario: One team is updating only the sales app’s forms and another team is updating custom entities and flows. They might ship separate solutions: one segmented for forms, the other for back-end changes, to avoid collisions and coordinate independently. 

🧠 Memory Trick: “Segment like slicing a pizza – only pick the slices you need 🍕.” Instead of delivering the whole pizza (whole table), you can deliver just the slice (specific forms/fields) your user asked for. Just don’t forget any toppings (dependent components) on the slice! 

📍 Practical Exercise – Creating a Segmented Solution: 

In a Dev solution, click Add existing → Table and choose a table (like Account). When the dialog appears with options, select “Edit objects”【11†L14-L22】. Pick just one or two assets, e.g., one Form and one View that you customized. Add them. 

Export the solution and import as managed into Test. Verify that only the selected form and view imported/updated, and other forms/views of Account remain untouched in Test (coming from prior solution or base). 

Try the opposite: Create another solution and add the same Account but pick “Include all objects”【11†L14-L22】. Importing that to Test will bring everything for Account (notice in the list of changes it might override things unexpectedly). This demonstrates why segmented approach (picking only needed objects) is safer for updates to existing tables. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: If you see exam mention of “table segmentation” or “segmented solutions,” recall it’s about reducing included components in a solution update【11†L12-L21】. A likely tricky question: “How can you avoid unintentionally overwriting an existing form when exporting a solution update?” The answer: only include the components that changed (use solution segmentation)【11†L25-L33】. Conversely, adding unmodified components can override them and create layers that might disable earlier customizations【11†L25-L33】. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Do not always include everything “just to be safe.” It’s tempting to add whole tables to solutions, but that can cause unintended side effects (over-layering). Instead, include only changed/new components whenever possible. This results in cleaner layering and fewer dependency issues【11†L25-L33】. However, make sure any prerequisite components (like a field needed by that form) are included or already present, to avoid import errors. Segmentation is a powerful technique – plan carefully which pieces each solution will carry. 

 

Part 12: Solution Dependencies 

What are Solution Dependencies? Dependencies are relationships where one component or solution requires another to function【12†L12-L20】. If component A references component B, then A depends on B. The Power Platform tracks these to prevent you from deleting something that’s needed by something else【12†L12-L20】. At a solution level, a managed solution can depend on components from another solution (especially common in layered or base+extension solutions)【8†L88-L96】. 

Required vs Optional Dependencies: 

Required dependencies are strict: Solution X must have Solution Y present first, or it cannot be installed (and conversely you cannot remove Y while X is still installed)【8†L88-L96】. These typically come from one solution customizing components from another (like a field on an entity from a base solution). 

Optional (internal) dependencies: Within a solution, components often depend on each other (like a form depends on fields). These are handled automatically during import (you’ll get prompted if something is missing that’s needed). 

Checking Dependencies: In the solution UI, use “Show Dependencies” on a component or the solution【12†L62-L70】. For example, if you select a form and click Show Dependencies, you might see that a field on it is required – meaning you can’t remove that field without removing it from the form first. Or if you try to delete a managed solution, it will list dependent components that are stopping it【12†L71-L79】. This list lets you identify what to address. 

“Cannot Delete” Errors: If you try to remove a component (or uninstall a solution) that has dependencies, you’ll get an error. Example: you try to delete a custom table but a flow in another solution uses that table – the system will block deletion until you remove or update that flow【12†L12-L20】【12†L78-L86】. The fix is to either remove that dependent component (e.g., remove the table reference from the flow) or delete the dependent first (uninstall the solution containing the flow), then remove the base component. 

Dependency Tracking in UI: When you see a dependency list, it shows Required Component vs Dependent Component columns – like “Table X (required by) Flow Y in Solution Z.” The Solution Layers view also helps identify cross-solution dependencies (e.g., one solution adding fields to an entity from another solution)【12†L85-L93】. 

📍 Practical Exercise – Checking Dependencies: 

In a solution, create a flow that uses a certain table or item (like sends an email using a specific email template record). Also have a app or something referencing that flow. 

Now try to delete that table or email template from the solution (in Maker portal). It should refuse, giving a “can’t delete because _______ depends on it”. Click Show Dependencies – you’ll see the list of components that depend on it (like the flow)【12†L78-L86】. 

Remove the dependency – e.g., delete the flow or remove that reference – then delete the component. 

For a solution-level view, try to uninstall a managed solution that has others built on top (if you set up a scenario from earlier parts with layering). The platform will block it until you remove the dependent solution first【12†L44-L52】【12†L48-L56】. 

⚠️ Exam Trap: Understand that dependencies can block solution uninstall or component deletion【12†L12-L20】. An exam question might describe a scenario: “You cannot delete a field because of dependencies – what do you do?” The correct approach is usually remove references first (like remove the field from forms, or delete flows referencing it) before deleting. Also remember, installed managed solutions can depend on each other – e.g., a “base solution” must be present for an “extension solution”, requiring correct order of import/uninstall【8†L88-L96】. 

💡 Consulting Tip: Before major deletes or refactoring, always use dependency check. The UI’s dependency checker is your friend – use it to see what will be affected by a change. In complex projects, document the dependencies between solutions (i.e., if Solution B extends Solution A, note that in documentation). This helps when planning upgrades or if you ever need to remove something. Also, keep things as loosely coupled as feasible – heavy inter-dependencies make ALM harder (for example, consider merging solutions or re-thinking architecture if there’s too much cross-dependence). 

 

Part 13: Power Platform Pipelines 

What are Pipelines? Power Platform Pipelines is a built-in ALM deployment capability inside Power Platform that provides a simple, low-code way to promote solutions from Dev to Test to Prod, aimed at making ALM accessible for all makers and admins【13†L14-L22】【13†L18-L26】. Instead of manually exporting/importing solutions, pipelines allow makers to click a “Deploy” button to push changes through predefined stages. 

How Pipelines Work (Overview): An admin configures a Pipeline in a dedicated “Pipeline Host” environment by defining stages (like Dev → Test → Prod, mapping to environment names) and using environment variables to store stage configurations【13†L18-L26】. Makers can then go to their solution in Dev, click Deploy (via Pipeline)【7†L67-L71】, and choose the pipeline stage (Test/Prod). The system will automatically export the solution (as managed) and import it to the target environment, including handling connection references and environment variables mapping up-front【13†L37-L40】. Approvals can be required for Prod stage if configured. 

Pipeline Stages (Dev → Test → Prod): Typically a pipeline has at least 3 stages: Development, QA/Test, Production – but you can configure accordingly, such as including a UAT stage. Each stage is linked to a actual environment(s) designated as Dev or target. 

Maker Experience vs Admin Experience: 

Admins: Set up pipelines using the Pipelines app (in preview) by installing the Pipelines Solution in a host environment. They define which environments are Dev vs Test vs Prod and any gating (like require approvals for Prod)【13†L22-L30】. Admins ensure all target environments are Managed Environments (often a requirement from 2026 onward for pipeline compliance)【13†L73-L81】【13†L85-L93】. 

Makers: Once pipelines are configured, in their Dev environment solutions, makers see a Deploy option. The UI guides them – e.g., shows if any dependency or missing connection issues before deployment【13†L35-L39】. Makers do not need deep ALM knowledge; they just pick a stage and deploy. The pipeline will automatically handle tasks like backing up the solution artifact, and mapping env variables and connections with minimal prompts【13†L35-L43】【13†L52-L60】. 

Benefits Over Manual Export/Import: 

Ease of use: No manual downloads/uploads; accessible to makers. 

Pre-validation: Pipelines check for missing dependencies or misconfigurations before importing, reducing failed deployments【13†L37-L40】. 

Speed: Fewer manual steps, consistent process every time. 

Governance: Admins can enforce certain controls (like approvals for Prod, only certain users can deploy, etc.). 

Traceability: Solution artifacts are automatically stored and logged for each pipeline run (so you have a backup of what was moved)【13†L64-L72】. 

Integration: Extensible via Power Platform CLI or Power Automate to integrate with other CI/CD if needed【13†L41-L49】. 

Pipelines vs. Azure DevOps vs. Manual – Comparison Table: 

 

